Object.assign(window.AVICENNIAN_CORPUS,{"analysis":[{"id":"ch01","kind":"analysis","author":"GNAMMA scholarly editorial synthesis","work":"Late Avicennian Summa · comparative chapters","title":"Mental existence and quiddity","discipline":"Ontology","locus":"Working chapter","lang":"mixed","family":"Comparative analysis","coverage":"Working analytical chapter with selected primary passages","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/gnamma-research/booksite/blob/master/research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/01-mental-existence-and-quiddity.md","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/01-mental-existence-and-quiddity.md","topics":["mental-existence","quiddity","knowledge"],"sourceCoverage":"Selected Arabic/Latin excerpts; no full primary witness","manuscriptCollated":false,"md":"# Book I, Chapter 1 — Mental existence, quiddity, and the meaning of *wujūd*\n**A post-classical Avicennian argument in full, with a critical comparison to Thomistic intentionality**\n\n**Editorial state:** **Translation of one complete bounded argument unit** in Lāhījī's *Shawāriq al-ilhām*, vol. I, printed p. 52 (fifth question, through but not including the heading of the sixth). The associated mental-existence discussion from pp. 50–52 is represented by selected lemmata, **not** a complete translation. One digital print transcription has been checked; no handwritten witnesses or alternative editions have been collated.\n\n**Primary sources:** [Lāhījī I:50](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/50?lang=ar), [I:51](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/51?lang=ar), [I:52](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/52?lang=ar); [Aquinas, *Summa theologiae* I q. 85 a. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html).\n\n## I. The problem\n\nWhen the intellect understands a horse, is the horse's **quiddity** present in intellect, or only a distinct representation? How can a mental content representing a substance be an accidental **quality** in the knower? And is existence a separate property added to an already constituted quiddity?\n\nThese are connected but not identical questions. Lāhījī's solution turns on an exact differentiation between **what is represented**, **the representation's mode of being in a knower**, and **the representation as an actual psychological event**. His subsequent criticism of existence as a real adjunct to a prior quiddity prevents an overly literal use of the essence–existence distinction.\n\n### A. A substance known and the cognition as a quality\n\n**Arabic — Lāhījī I:50 (selected contiguous passage)**\n\n> فهو من حيث هو معلوم بالذات وجوهر أي ماهية جوهر وكلي وموجود في الذهن، ومن حيث خصوص الوجود الذهني علم، ومن حيث القيام بالذهن الشخصي والمحفوفية بعوارض ذلك الذهن جزئي وعرض من الكيفيات النفسانية وموجود خارجي بالمعنى المقابل للوجود الظلي.\n\n**Translation**\n\nConsidered in itself, it is something known primarily, a substance—that is, it has the quiddity of a substance—a universal, and something existing in the mind. Considered with regard to the specific mode of its mental existence, it is knowledge. Considered insofar as it inheres in a particular mind and is surrounded by that mind's individual conditions, it is a particular, an accident belonging to the psychological qualities, and an externally existing thing in the sense opposed to derivative or shadowy existence.\n\n**Critical note 1.** *Mawlūm bi-l-dhāt* must not be collapsed into the accidentally cognised extramental individual, *maʿlūm bi-l-ʿaraḍ*. That the mental act is an accidental quality does not imply that the object signified thereby is a quality.\n\n**Critical note 2.** The word \"external\" in the last clause means **the act of cognition as an actual state of the really existing knower**, not that the horse represented exists extramentally inside the mind. Lāhījī explicitly differentiates these descriptions.\n\n### B. The apparent contradiction is equivocation between respects\n\n**Arabic — I:50 (selected)**\n\n> ولا منافاة بين كون الشيء جوهرا ذهنيا وعرضا خارجيا ... وإنما المنافاة بين كون الشيء جوهرا ذهنيا وعرضا ذهنيا أو بين كونه جوهرا خارجيا وعرضا خارجيا.\n\n**Translation**\n\nThere is no contradiction in something being a mental substance and an extramental accident. What would be contradictory is its being both mental substance and mental accident, or both extramental substance and extramental accident, *in the same consideration*.\n\nHere \"mental substance\" means that the content represents something whose extramental mode of being is not inherence in a subject; \"extramental accident\" means that the representing psychological state actually inheres in a knower.\n\n### C. The knowing image itself is actual in the knower\n\n**Arabic — I:51 (selected)**\n\n> ومما يجب أن يعلم أن وجود الصورة العلمية في الذهن وقيامها به إنما هو وجود ذهني للمعلوم الذي هو ذو الصورة وليس هو وجودا ذهنيا لنفس هذه الصورة ... فإن الذهن أعني النفس الناطقة موجودة في الخارج وقد قامت بها تلك الصورة وترتبت على تلك الصورة آثارها ولوازمها.\n\n**Translation**\n\nOne must understand that the existence of the cognitive form in the mind, and its inherence there, constitutes the mental existence of what is known—the thing represented by that form—not the mental existence of the form considered as an object in its own right. For the mind, that is, the rational soul, really exists outside the mind as an actual being; the cognitive form inheres in it, and the form's own effects and concomitants belong to it.\n\n**Source caveat.** The sentence includes material omitted by the ellipsis; it has been broken out as an *explanatory excerpt*, not a continuous translation of every word on p. 51.\n\n### D. Why a likeness is not necessarily a substitute object\n\nLāhījī reports a customary distinction between ancient advocates of mental \"images\" resembling objects and later writers who speak of the quiddities themselves obtaining mentally. He argues that the word \"shadow\" can designate a quiddity's **different mode of being**, without implying a fundamentally different object merely matching some of its accidents.\n\n**Arabic — I:52 (selected)**\n\n> فإن الحكم على شيء إنما يستدعي وجود ذلك الشيء وثبوته لا ثبوت أمر مغاير له وإن وافقه في بعض الأعراض.\n\n**Translation**\n\nFor judging something requires the being or establishment of that very thing, not the establishment of something numerically or essentially different from it merely because the latter resembles it in certain accidents.\n\n**Limitation:** This does not yet answer all problems of intentionality, nor settle whether the known nature is numerically identical across mental and external instantiations. The relevant *Shifāʾ* and logical discussions must be collated.\n\n---\n\n## II. COMPLETE BOUNDED TRANSLATION: *Shawāriq* I:52, the fifth question\n\n**Boundary:** from «المسألة الخامسة [في أن الوجود ليس أمرا ينضم إلى الماهية]» through the final sentence immediately before «المسألة السادسة». Arabic orthography and punctuation below are lightly normalised; the underlying online print transcription has not been collated.\n\n### Arabic base passage\n\n> **المسألة الخامسة:** في أن الوجود ليس أمراً ينضم إلى الماهية فيتحقق به الماهية، بل هو نفس تحقق الماهية وحصولها وكونها كما أشرنا إليه فيما سبق، وإليه أشار المصنف بقوله: «وليس الوجود معنى به يحصل الماهية في العين بل الحصول».\n>\n> وهذا ردّ لما ذهب إليه جماعة من أتباع المشّائين من كون الوجود صفة موجودة في الخارج منضمة إلى الماهية وكون الماهية موجودة بها؛ فعندهم المجعول بالذات والصادر من الجاعل بالحقيقة إنما هو الوجود، والماهية من حيث هي غير مجعولة ومن حيث الاتصاف بالوجود مجعولة بالعرض. وهذا المذهب بظاهره سخيف جداً، وقد أبطله شيخ الإشراق في كتاب الإشراق وغيره بما لا مزيد عليه.\n>\n> وحاصله أن ثبوت الصفة العينية للموصوف يتوقف على ثبوت الموصوف في العين، فيلزم كون الماهية موجودة في الخارج قبل وجودها في الخارج. وأيضاً لو كان الوجود موجوداً في الخارج فإما بوجود غيره فيلزم التسلسل في الوجودات، وإما بوجود هو نفسه فيلزم أن لا يكون حمل الموجود على الوجود وغيره بمعنى واحد، إذ مفهومه فيه أنه نفس الوجود وفي غيره أنه شيء له الوجود، والحال أنه لا يطلق على الجميع إلا بمعنى واحد.\n>\n> ومستندهم هو أن الماهية إن لم ينضم إليها أمر من الجاعل فهي على العدم. وجوابه أنكم فرضتم ماهية في الخارج ثم يضم الجاعل الوجود إليها، وهذا باطل؛ لأن الماهية إذا كانت أولاً فهي موجودة، إذ لا يفهم من الوجود إلا كون الماهية، فأي حاجة إلى ضم الوجود إليها؟ بل نفس الماهية إنما صدرت من الجاعل فكانت في الخارج، فكان كونها في الخارج بعد الصدور هو وجودها.\n>\n> والمصنف أشار بهذا الكلام إلى أنه لا حاجة في إبطال ذلك المذهب إلى هذه المؤونة، بل يكفي أن يقال: إنا لا نعني ولا نفهم من الوجود إلا كون الماهية وحصولها بالضرورة، لا أمراً ينضم إلى الماهية فتصير موجودة؛ فلو انضم إليها أمر من الجاعل لا يكون ذلك الأمر وجوداً بالمعنى المفهوم للكل.\n>\n> ولا ينبغي أن يتوهم المنافاة بين ما ذكر هاهنا وبين ما أشرنا إليه سابقاً وسيأتي تحقيقه من كون الوجود عند الحكماء ذا أفراد حقيقية، إذ ليست تلك الأفراد عندهم ما ينضم إلى الماهية فتصير موجودة به، بل إن هي إلا أكوان خاصة للماهيات المتخالفة، كل منها نفس كون ماهية مخصوصة بحسب الخارج، كما ستعرفه إن شاء الله تعالى.\n\n### English — complete translation of the argument unit\n\n**The fifth question: Existence is not something that attaches to a quiddity such that the quiddity comes to be established through it; rather, it is the very establishment, obtaining, and being of that quiddity.**\n\nWe have already indicated this, and it is what the author of the base text signifies when he says: “Existence is not a meaning through which a quiddity obtains in extramental reality; rather, it is the obtaining itself.”\n\nThis replies to the position of a group among the followers of the Peripatetics: according to them, existence is a really existing attribute that is joined to a quiddity outside the mind, such that the quiddity becomes existent through it. On their account, what is made in itself, what genuinely proceeds from the productive cause, is existence; the quiddity, considered in itself, is not made, while considered as qualified by existence it is made incidentally. In its apparent sense this position is extremely unsatisfactory. The Master of Illumination has refuted it in the *Ḥikmat al-ishrāq* and elsewhere in a way that leaves little to add.\n\nThe substance of the refutation is as follows. For a real attribute to belong to a subject, that subject must already be established in reality. It would therefore follow that a quiddity is existent outside the mind **before** it exists there. Moreover, if existence itself were something existing outside the mind, either it would exist through another existence, in which case an infinite regress of existences would result; or it would exist through an existence identical with itself, in which case predicating “existent” of existence and of something else would not have the same meaning. For in the former case it would mean “existence itself,” and in the latter “something possessing existence,” whereas the expression is employed with one common meaning.\n\nThe ground invoked by advocates of that position is that a quiddity remains non-existent unless something coming from the productive cause is added to it. The reply is that you have first posited a quiddity outside the mind and then had the producer add existence to it. That is false: if the quiddity is already there in the first place, it is already existent. For by existence we understand nothing other than the quiddity's being; what need, then, would there be to adjoin existence to it? Rather, the quiddity itself proceeds from the productive cause and is thereby outside the mind; its being outside the mind upon proceeding from that cause **is its existence**.\n\nThe author of the base text indicates that this more elaborate effort is unnecessary for refuting the disputed position. It suffices to say that by existence we necessarily mean and understand nothing but the quiddity's being and obtaining, not something that is joined to it and thereby makes it existent. Even if something were adjoined to it by the producer, that addition would not be existence in the meaning commonly understood by everyone.\n\nNor should anyone imagine a contradiction between the present account and the view already mentioned, which we shall examine further, that for the philosophers existence has **real instances**. For those instances are not, on their account, things that join a quiddity and thereby make it exist. They are nothing other than **particular modes of being of different quiddities**, each being precisely the extramental actuality of a determinate quiddity, as you shall learn, God willing.\n\n### Critical notes on this translated unit\n\n**[E1] Attribution.** The terse sentence introduced by «قال المصنف» is the *Tajrīd* lemma being expounded. The criticisms involving Suhrawardī, the explanatory regress, and the final reconciliation of particular existences are **Lāhījī's commentary**. They must not be merged into a single authorial voice.\n\n**[E2] Scope of criticism.** The target is an **extrinsically joined, independently actual property** called existence. The argument does *not* simply deny that there can be a genuine metaphysical distinction between essence and *esse*, and it does not establish the Scotistic or Thomistic status of that distinction.\n\n**[E3] The apparent contradiction resolved.** He denies “existence is a second actual item joined to a previously actual essence” while affirming “there are different particular existences.” That is consistent if the latter are the *ways in which different things actually are*, not a stock of detachable existence-properties.\n\n**[E4] What follows concerning Ṣadrā?** Neither the primacy of existence (*aṣālat al-wujūd*), nor a single all-inclusive reality of existence, follows merely from this critique of the extrinsic-property account. A separate ontology is needed.\n\n**[E5] Editorial uncertainty.** The online copy's transcription has occasional spelling and typographical irregularities. No manuscript variants are being reported or invented; the Arabic here is a normalised reading of that one printing.\n\n---\n\n## III. The Thomistic comparison: *species qua*, not *species quod*\n\nAquinas distinguishes the intelligible form as **that by which** the intellect understands from the extramental nature as **what** the intellect primarily understands. He rejects the thesis that knowledge of nature is restricted to cognition of one's own mental representations. See *Summa theologiae* I q. 85 a. 2, [Corpus Thomisticum](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html), response and replies.\n\n**Latin lemma:**\n\n> *Species intelligibilis non est quod intelligitur actu, sed id quo intelligit intellectus.*\n\n**Translation:**\n\nThe intelligible species is not what is actually understood; rather, it is that by which the intellect understands.\n\nThis is **not automatically** equivalent to Lāhījī's distinction. The latter employs an elaborate system of “mental existence of what is known” and “external existence of the mental act,” whereas Aquinas's formal/objective distinction involves a theory of intentional assimilation, abstraction, and the act's term. The comparison must preserve these technical differences.\n\n### Argument graph\n\n1. The intellect can consider **the quiddity** of an extramental substance.\n2. The mental act is itself a **particular quality** belonging to a particular intellect.\n3. It is not a contradiction that the quiddity **represented** belongs to the category substance while the **act representing it** belongs to the category quality.\n4. Therefore the existence of a representation must not be mistaken for an extramental horse or other substance somehow lodged in the intellect.\n5. In the linked ontology, “existence” means the actual being/obtaining of a thing and need not be pictured as an additional actual item glued to it.\n6. Neither (1)–(5) proves the numerical identity of nature across instantiations, nor settles whether existence is really or only conceptually distinct from essence.\n7. Both the Avicennian and Thomistic accounts therefore require a further analysis of **intentionality and metaphysical composition**.\n\n### New result for the mature-summa project\n\nLāhījī is **not** simply a conservative repeater of a naive essence-plus-existence-as-accident theory. His text already integrates a theory of intentionality, the critique of object-substitution, differentiated existence, and a refined interpretation of making. The next complete argument unit, [*On what a cause makes*](02-making-and-causal-explanation.md), shows why that last topic cannot be detached from the foregoing distinctions.\n\n**Status audit:** complete translation of the *fifth question* on p. 52; selective excerpts only of the wider mental-existence argument pp. 50–52; comparison with Aquinas source-located, but variant collation and full Avicennian *Shifāʾ* witnesses pending.\n"},{"id":"ch02","kind":"analysis","author":"GNAMMA scholarly editorial synthesis","work":"Late Avicennian Summa · comparative chapters","title":"Making and causal explanation","discipline":"Metaphysics","locus":"Working chapter","lang":"mixed","family":"Comparative analysis","coverage":"Working analytical chapter with selected primary passages","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/gnamma-research/booksite/blob/master/research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/02-making-and-causal-explanation.md","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/02-making-and-causal-explanation.md","topics":["existence","causation","quiddity"],"sourceCoverage":"Selected Arabic/Latin excerpts; no full primary witness","manuscriptCollated":false,"md":"# Book I, Chapter 2 — *Jaʿl*: what does the cause actually make?\n**Lāhījī, *Shawāriq al-ilhām* I:158–161; al-Taftāzānī, al-Ījī and al-Jurjānī; comparison with Ibn Sīnā and Aquinas**\n\n**Scholarly status:** A connected critical translation of the central **objection–reply sequence** on printed pp. 158–161. All specifically translated Arabic extracts are identified below; the page contains additional technical discussion of the classification of simple and complex essences, which is **not** represented as exhaustively translated. Arabic checked against the named searchable print transcription only. No source-manuscript variants established.\n\n**Primary sources:** [Shawāriq I:158](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/158?lang=ar), [I:159](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/159?lang=ar), [I:160](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/160?lang=ar), [I:161](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/161?lang=ar). The cited *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid* and *Sharḥ al-Mawāqif* are **embedded quotations reported by Lāhījī**; they must be checked against independent editions before being represented as collated readings.\n\n## Thesis and the prior question\n\nThe dispute over whether quiddities are “made” often mixes up at least four questions:\n\n1. Does **an existent thing** owe its existence to a producer?\n2. Does a thing's **quiddity count as the kind of quiddity it is** only through an act of production?\n3. Does a **complex** quiddity have parts and thus a kind of internal dependence not shared by something simple?\n4. What is really one outside the mind but analysed into distinct concepts—**particular existent, its quiddity, and its existence**?\n\nThe disputants can agree on (1) while disagreeing about (2)–(4), or appear to disagree only because they use “made” differently.\n\n## I. Three reported positions — *Shawāriq* I:158\n\n**Arabic (the reported schools)**\n\n> وكما يتحقق الحاجة إلى جاعل في المركب فكذا في البسيط. اعلم أن العقلاء بعد الاتفاق على أن وجود الممكن بالفاعل اختلفوا في ماهيته. قال شارح المقاصد: فذهب المتكلمون إلى أنها بجعل الجاعل مطلقاً، أي بسيطة كانت ومركبة، وذهب جمهور الفلاسفة والمعتزلة إلى أنها ليست بجعل الجاعل، بمعنى أن شيئاً منها ليس بمجعول، وذهب بعضهم إلى أن المركبات مجعولة دون البسائط.\n\n**Translation**\n\nJust as there is a need for a maker in the case of something composite, so there is in the case of something simple. Understand that, although those who employ reason agree that the **existence** of a contingent thing comes from an agent, they disagree about its **quiddity**. The commentator on the *Maqāṣid* [al-Taftāzānī] states: the theologians maintained that it comes about through the producer's making, whether simple or composite; the majority of the philosophers and the Muʿtazilites maintained that it does not come about through the producer's making, in the sense that none of it is made; and some maintained that composite quiddities are made but simple quiddities are not.\n\n**Editorial qualification:** These are historical *position ascriptions in this dispute*, not a blanket agreement among all theologians or all Muʿtazila. The strong Muʿtazilī view about the nonexistent as a thing must be checked school by school.\n\n### A. The missing distinction: simple and composite making\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> وأما وجه ذهاب الفلاسفة إليه فهو أنهم نفوا الجعل المركب، أعني جعل الماهية ماهية، على ما يدل عليه دليلهم كما يأتي. وأما الجعل البسيط، أعني كون الصادر عن الفاعل نفس الماهية ...\n\n**Translation**\n\nThe reason for attributing that view to the philosophers is that they deny **composite making**: making a quiddity be that very quiddity, as their proof will indicate. As for **simple making**, meaning that the very quiddity is what proceeds from the agent ...\n\n**The passage continues on p. 159.** The ellipsis above indicates omitted text, not a completed paragraph. The real distinction is between “making something exist” and “making humanity be humanity” or “making blackness be blackness.”\n\n## II. The first reported position: its three arguments and Lāhījī's replies — I:159\n\n### Objection 1: possibility is universal\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> ثم إن مستند المذهب الأول أمور: الأول أن علة الحاجة هي الإمكان وهي عامة.\n\n**Translation**\n\nThe first position rests on several considerations. First, the ground of causal dependence is possibility, which is common [to the simple and the composite].\n\n**Answer**\n\n> والجواب عن الأول بأن معنى حاجة الممكن إلى الجاعل أن وجوده منه لا ماهيته.\n\nThe answer to the first is that the need of the contingent being for a producer means that **its existence comes from that producer**, not that its quiddity as such comes from him in the sense under dispute.\n\n### Objection 2: if the quiddity is unmade, making disappears altogether\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> الثاني أنه لو لم يكن الماهية مجعولة ارتفع المجعولية؛ لأن ما فرض كونه مجعولاً من الوجود أو موصوفية الماهية به فهو أيضاً ماهيته في نفسه، والمفروض أن ما هو ماهية ليس بمجعول.\n\n**Translation**\n\nSecond, if quiddity were not made, making as such would disappear. For whatever is assumed to be made—existence or the quiddity's being qualified by existence—also has a quiddity of its own, and the assumption is that whatever is a quiddity is not made.\n\n**Answer**\n\n> وعن الثاني أن المجعول هو الوجود الخاص وما هو من أفراد الوجود، لا ماهية الوجود ومفهومه.\n\nIn reply to the second: what is made is **particular existence**, something that belongs to the instances of existence—not the *quiddity* or *concept* of existence.\n\n**Critical distinction:** Lāhījī's reply permits particular actuality to be produced while rejecting the proposition that the **meaning** of existence has to be made a meaning. Compare [Chapter 1](01-mental-existence-and-quiddity.md) on why existence is not an independently actual property adjoined to an already actual quiddity.\n\n### Objection 3: without making, a quiddity would possess independent external establishment\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> الثالث أنه لا تقرر للماهية في الخارج بذاتها، لما مر في نفي ثبوت المعدومات، فيكون تقررها بالجاعل.\n\n**Translation**\n\nThird, the quiddity has no independent establishment outside the mind, as already explained when denying the established reality of non-existent things. Its actual establishment must therefore come from a producer.\n\n**Answer**\n\n> وعن الثالث أنه ليس تقرر الماهية إلا وجودها.\n\nIn reply to the third: the quiddity's external establishment is **nothing other than its existence**.\n\n**Result:** His thesis does not posit a quiddity established outside the mind awaiting a separate act of existential attachment. It is precisely this possible misconception that must be removed in comparing him with Aquinas.\n\n## III. The opposing proof from essential identity — I:159\n\n**Arabic (reported proof and counter-reply)**\n\n> ومستند المذهب الثاني أن كون الإنسان إنساناً مثلاً لو كان بجعل الجاعل لارتفع بارتفاعه فيلزم سلب الشيء عن نفسه على تقدير عدم الفاعل وهو محال. وأجابوا عنه بمنع استحالته على طريق السلب دون العدول. وردّ بأن السلب الصادق على الإنسان حال ارتفاعه هو أن المعدوم ليس بإنسان لا أن الإنسان ليس بإنسان، والحق أن استحالة سلب الشيء عن نفسه ضرورية.\n\n**Translation**\n\nThe argument supporting the second position is that, if a human being's being human, for example, resulted from an agent's making it so, then it would cease to be human if that productive activity were removed. This would entail denying of a thing what it itself is, on the hypothesis of the agent's absence—which is impossible.\n\nThe reply offered to this says that there is no impossibility here when the denial is taken as **negative predication** rather than as the affirmation of an opposite property [*salb* as opposed to *ʿudūl*]. But this is answered as follows: the true negation when the human is absent is “the non-existent is not a human,” not “the human is not human.” The impossibility of denying of something what it itself is must be regarded as necessary.\n\n**Apparatus:** The distinction between *salb* and *ʿudūl* is a technical logical distinction, not evidence that one can coherently predicate “not human” of an actually existing human. The objection guards the invariance of an essence's defining content under hypothetical non-instantiation.\n\n## IV. A third position: composites but not simple quiddities — I:159\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> ومستند المذهب الثالث أن شرط المجعولية الإمكان وهو يستدعي نسبة وهي تستدعي اثنينية وهي غير متصورة في البسيط دون المركب. والجواب أن الاثنينية التي تستدعيها النسبة اللازمة للإمكان إنما هي باعتبار الماهية والوجود وهي شاملة للبسيط والمركب.\n\n**Translation**\n\nThe third position rests on the claim that the condition of being made is possibility; possibility requires a relation, which in turn requires a duality, and such duality cannot be conceived in the simple, though it can be conceived in the composite.\n\nThe reply is that the duality required by the relation involved in possibility is the distinction **in consideration between quiddity and existence**. It applies to both the simple and the composite.\n\n**Key inference:** Dependence is not restricted to internally composite essences; an externally dependent simple being can still be contingent. This matters when drawing analogies to separated intellects or incorporeal created substances.\n\n## V. Al-Taftāzānī: clarify the *real* subject of disagreement — I:159\n\nLāhījī quotes the commentator on the *Maqāṣid*. We reproduce the argument as **reported in Lāhījī**; independent collation against *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid* is outstanding.\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> فإنه معلوم أن ليس للفاعل تأثير وجعل بالنسبة إلى ماهية الممكن وآخر بالنسبة إلى الوجود حتى تكون الماهية مجعولة كالوجود، وأن ليس للماهية تقرر في الخارج بدون الفاعل حتى يكون المجعول هو الوجود فقط، بل أثر الفاعل مجعولية الماهية بمعنى صيرورتها موجودة.\n\n**Translation**\n\nFor it is known that the agent does not exercise one act of influence and making with respect to the contingent being's quiddity and a **second** act with respect to its existence, such that the quiddity is made just as existence is made. Nor does the quiddity enjoy extramental establishment without the agent, such that only existence is made. Rather, the agent's effect is that the quiddity becomes actual—that is, it is made in the sense of **coming to exist**.\n\n**Critical insight:** Taftāzānī does not present essence and existence as two separately manufactured external items. This is an important point of contact with an AT analysis of the composite's dependence on a cause.\n\n## VI. Al-Ījī and Jurjānī: two senses of dependence — I:159–160\n\nLāhījī next relays the author of the *Mawāqif* and the commentator on it. Do not attribute all the following distinctions directly to al-Ījī.\n\n**Arabic — reported in I:159**\n\n> المجعولية قد يراد بها الاحتياج إلى الفاعل وقد يراد بها الاحتياج إلى الغير على ما يعم الجزء.\n\n**Translation**\n\n“Being made” may mean needing an **efficient agent**, or it may mean needing **another** in a broader sense that includes needing a constitutive part.\n\nA simple contingent existent can need an efficient agent without being composed of internal parts; a composite quiddity additionally depends upon its constituents. Treating these as the same need would make the disagreement appear larger than it is.\n\n**Arabic — I:160, reported *Sharḥ al-Mawāqif***\n\n> والصواب أن يقال معنى قولهم الماهية ليست مجعولة أنها في حد ذاتها لا يتعلق بها جعل جاعل وتأثير مؤثر ... فلا مغايرة بين الماهية ونفسها حتى يتصور توسط جعل بينهما ...\n\n**Translation**\n\nThe correct way to state the meaning of “the quiddity is not made” is that, taken in itself, it is not the terminus of a producer's making or a cause's influence. There is no difference between a quiddity and itself such that one could conceive an act of making that intervened between the two.\n\nThis does not mean the **existent** is independent of its creator. It means a causal act is not required to make what humanity means *be* humanity.\n\n### The dyer analogy — I:160\n\n**Arabic — reported *Sharḥ al-Mawāqif***\n\n> فإن الصباغ مثلاً إذا صبغ ثوباً فإنه لا يجعل الثوب ثوباً ولا الصبغ صبغاً بل يجعل الثوب متصفاً بالصبغ في الخارج ...\n\n**Translation**\n\nWhen a dyer dyes a garment, he does not make the garment be a garment, nor dye be dye; he makes the garment qualified by the dye outside the mind.\n\n**Lāhījī's criticism**\n\n> أقول وهذا كلام لا غبار عليه إلا أنه ينبغي تحقيق معنى جعل الماهية متصفة بالوجود لئلا يتوهم أن الماهية والوجود كليهما حاصلان بلا جعل وأثر الجاعل أنه يجعل أحدهما متصفاً بالآخر.\n\nI say: this is a sound account, **except** that we must clarify what is meant by making a quiddity qualified by existence, lest someone imagine that quiddity and existence are both already actual without being made and that the producer's effect is merely to join one to the other.\n\nThe dyer analogy is pedagogical, not an ontological model of two already established substances being combined. That further clarification is Lāhījī's central corrective.\n\n## VII. Lāhījī's own adjudication — I:161\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> بل المراد أن الصادر من الجاعل إنما هو أمر واحد يحلله العقل إلى أمرين ويجعل أحدهما متصفاً بالآخر، فهذا هو معنى جعل الماهية متصفة بالوجود كما مر غير مرة.\n\n**Translation**\n\nRather, the intention is that what proceeds from the producer is **one thing**, which the intellect analyses into two considerations and regards one as qualified by the other. **That** is the meaning of making a quiddity qualified by existence, as has already been explained more than once.\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> ثم أقول لكن هذا الكلام أيضاً يرفع النزاع فلم يحصل معنى يصلح للخلاف، بل الصواب كما أشرنا إليه أن يجعل النزاع في الجعل المركب للماهية مع قطع النظر عن الوجود، فإن الاحتياج إلى الجاعل في الوجود موضع الوفاق كما عرفت والجعل البسيط للماهية دقيق لا يفهمه الأكثرون ...\n\n**Translation**\n\nI add that this account, too, removes the disagreement: no meaning remains that would properly support a dispute. The correct course, as we indicated, is to locate the disagreement in **composite making of the quiddity while abstracting from existence**. For the need of a producer in respect of existence is admitted on all sides, as has been shown, while the simple making of a quiddity is a subtle matter not understood by most.\n\nHe goes on to explain why some theologians, unfamiliar with simple making, treated making as composite; others, notably those who accepted pre-existential quiddities, denied the need to make those quiddities; philosophers denied the same compound making as unintelligible; and advocates of a simple/composite distinction reasoned from the imagined internal division of a composite. These are **Lāhījī's explanatory diagnoses of the schools**, not independent verification of what every author held.\n\n**Arabic — concluding interpretation**\n\n> فالماهيات الممكنة سواء كانت مركبة أو بسيطة محتاجة إليه على السواء ومجعولة بالجعل البسيط بالمعنى الذي عرفت.\n\n**Translation**\n\nContingent quiddities, whether composite or simple, need the producer equally and are made through **simple making** in the sense already explained.\n\n### The major repair to the prior argument\n\nThe productive act does not connect two already actual parts called \"essence\" and \"existence.\" It makes one **particular existent**, which the intellect legitimately considers under both the terms **quiddity** and **existence**. This is why Lāhījī's earlier claim that particular existence is what is made is consistent with rejecting a separately joined existential attribute.\n\nThis is a real **post-classical argumentative refinement**: it integrates the ontological problem with the disputed meanings of *jaʿl*, the logic of essential identity, and the three theological schools' senses of dependence.\n\n---\n\n## VIII. Where this places the AT comparison\n\nAquinas, in [*De potentia* III.8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/qdp3.html), says that what is produced in natural generation **per se** is the composite, while the form is that *through whose acquisition* something becomes actual; he denies that a natural substantial form must be individually created as an independent item.\n\nThis is not an identity of doctrines with Lāhījī. Yet both insist on a useful principle:\n\n**Do not multiply independently existing objects simply because an adequate explanation distinguishes several metaphysical principles.**\n\nThe points requiring independent investigation are:\n\n- Does the maker confer an act of existence really distinct from essence in Aquinas's sense, or is the essence–existence contrast in Lāhījī primarily a legitimate conceptual analysis of one actual existent?\n- Is a distinct natural **generator** the proximate cause of the composite's substantial form, or are proximate material agents merely dispositive with respect to the form's reception from a separate intellect?\n- How can the distinction between existence and quiddity be real **without** reifying both as prior existing items?\n- Does a simple created intellect have a composition of essence and existence without the material composition of parts?\n- How does the objection to real, separately added existence interact with Ṣadrā's *aṣālat al-wujūd* and *tashkīk*?\n\n### Dependency summary\n\n**Knowledge and quiddity → distinguishing mental and extramental modes → meaning of existence → particular existence as made → denial of an extrinsically attached existence-thing → simple making of one actual existent → real causal dependence without dependence on internal parts → generation and conservation.**\n\nThis is one continuous philosophical route, not a list of unrelated school positions.\n\n**Completion audit:** the fundamental positions, three objections, replies, reported Taftāzānī/Ījī/Jurjānī interventions and Lāhījī's decisive adjudication are translated above. Some connecting sentences and the extended technical treatment of simplicity and composition in the surrounding pages remain untranslated. No manuscript variant comparison has been made and the reported quotes must be collated against the original *Maqāṣid* and *Mawāqif* editions before any definitive apparatus is claimed.\n"},{"id":"ch03","kind":"analysis","author":"GNAMMA scholarly editorial synthesis","work":"Late Avicennian Summa · comparative chapters","title":"Substantial motion and persistence","discipline":"Natural philosophy","locus":"Working chapter","lang":"mixed","family":"Comparative analysis","coverage":"Working analytical chapter with selected primary passages","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/gnamma-research/booksite/blob/master/research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/03-substantial-motion-dispute.md","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/03-substantial-motion-dispute.md","topics":["motion","natural-generation","matter"],"sourceCoverage":"Selected Arabic/Latin excerpts; no full primary witness","manuscriptCollated":false,"md":"# Book II, Chapter 1 — Substantial motion: the persisting subject versus continuous existence\n**A direct comparison of al-Ḥillī, Lāhījī, Ṣadrā, Ibn Sīnā and the AT analysis of change**\n\n**Research state:** One *complete bounded argument unit* of Lāhījī (*Shawāriq* I:53, sixth question, through before the seventh question) rendered in connected English below; the Arabic original is given in selected, source-located lemmata, not reprinted in its entirety. Ṣadrā's principal counterargument in *Asfār* IV:85–87 is translated in connected portions with explicit omissions. The earliest al-Ḥillī witnesses are a source-located preliminary stage, not a complete translation of his many proofs. Single digital editions have been checked; manuscripts and textual variants remain outstanding.\n\n**Primary source links:** [Lāhījī I:52](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/52?lang=ar), [I:53](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/53?lang=ar); [Ṣadrā, *Asfār* IV:85](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/1606/85?lang=ar), [IV:86](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/1606/86?lang=ar), [IV:87](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/1606/87?lang=ar); [al-Ḥillī, *Nihāyat al-marām* IV:399](https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D9%86%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%85-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%85/v/4/p/399), [IV:410](https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D9%86%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%85-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%85/v/4/p/410).\n\n## I. The older, not merely reactive, Peripatetic problem\n\nBefore Ṣadrā, the general Aristotelian–Avicennian position distinguished:\n\n- **Accidental motion**, such as the same body becoming hotter or changing place;\n- **Substantial generation/corruption**, in which one substantial form ceases and another begins, ordinarily not analysed as continuous motion through an infinity of actual intermediate substantial species.\n\nThis distinction is explicitly argued in al-Ḥillī's *Nihāyat al-marām*, IV, discussion of the impossibility of motion in substance.\n\n**Arabic — al-Ḥillī IV:399**\n\n> اعلم أن الذي يقع الاشتباه فيه من الجواهر وأنه متحرك في الجوهر إنما هو الصور الجوهرية ... والمدّعى هنا بيان أن الصور الجوهرية إنما يمكن حدوثها على الوجه الأول دون الثاني.\n\n**Translation**\n\nKnow that the question susceptible of confusion among substances, whether something moves in substance, concerns substantial forms. The thesis to be established is that substantial forms can come into existence in the first of two ways—at once—and not in the second, namely gradually.\n\n**Arabic — IV:410**\n\n> أن هذا التبدل والتغير في الصور الطبيعية لا يمكن أن يحدث على التدريج، بل إنما يحدث دفعة واحدة.\n\n**Translation**\n\nSuch replacement or alteration of natural forms cannot occur gradually; it occurs all at once.\n\n**Important source distinction:** The web edition of IV:399 **interleaves a modern editor's note** about Ṣadrā and Ṭabāṭabāʾī with al-Ḥillī's primary prose. That note cannot be translated as if al-Ḥillī, who predated Ṣadrā, were discussing Ṣadrā's later theory. Its provenance belongs to the apparatus, not the matn.\n\nThe later disagreement is therefore more precise than “philosophy was static until Ṣadrā.” Many accidental movements, natural developments, and even substantial coming-to-be were already recognised; the disagreement concerns **the ontological category in which gradual change occurs** and whether the substance itself is an intrinsically flowing existence.\n\n## II. Lāhījī — sixth question on movement in existence (*Shawāriq* I:52–53)\n\n**Boundary:** from the heading «المسألة السادسة في أن الماهية لا تتحرك في الوجود» on p. 52 through the immediately preceding argument before «المسألة السابعة» on p. 53.\n\n### L1. Motion presupposes a subject stable relative to its changing determination\n\n**Arabic — p. 53**\n\n> والدليل على ذلك هو أن الحركة في كل شيء معناها أن يكون المتحرك متقوماً وموجوداً من دون ذلك الشيء وباقياً بعينه ويتوارد عليه في كل آن من الآنات المفروضة في زمان الحركة فرد من أفراد ذلك الشيء.\n\n**Translation**\n\nThe argument is that motion in respect of something means that the thing moving is constituted and exists without that respect in which it moves, remaining the same individual while a different instance of the relevant determination comes upon it at each posited instant during the motion.\n\nIn change of place, the body already exists without possessing this specific place and acquires successive positions. In heating, the same water persists while particular intensities of heat succeed one another.\n\n### L2. Essence cannot remain actual while one existence is exchanged for another\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> وظاهر أن مثل ذلك التبدل والتوارد لا يمكن في الماهية بالقياس إلى الوجود، فإن الماهية لا يتصور كونها متقومة وموجودة وباقية من دون الوجود ليتصور توارد الوجود عليها.\n\n**Translation**\n\nPlainly, such exchange and succession cannot occur to a quiddity with respect to existence, since a quiddity cannot be conceived as constituted, existent and persisting **without existence**, so that successive existences could come upon it.\n\nIn the same way, prime matter cannot undergo a motion *in* substantial form if it cannot be established without some form; the allegedly stable subject is not available in the way required by the definition of motion.\n\nThe inference extends to substance, essence and what is essential, and more generally to anything without which the subject cannot exist as a constituted actual thing.\n\n### L3. Gradation of different existents does not itself prove movement of one existent\n\nLāhījī grants the possibility of gradational comparison (*tashkīk*) among existing beings while resisting the inference from such comparison to the progressive intensification of **one** quiddity's existence. Different instances can be stronger and weaker without it following that the **same actual individual** changes continuously from one instance of existence into another.\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> وأيضاً إن الوجود ليس له فرد قائم بالماهية بمعنى كونه صفة موجودة ممتازة عن الماهية، بل هو مجرد كون الماهية.\n\n**Translation**\n\nMoreover, existence does not have an instance subsisting in a quiddity as though it were an actual property distinct from the quiddity; rather, it is simply the quiddity's being.\n\nThis links the substantial-motion problem directly to [the critique of an attached existence-property](01-mental-existence-and-quiddity.md).\n\n### L4. The first objection: perhaps existences can replace one another instantaneously\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> فإن قيل ... فيجوز أن يتوارد على الماهية وجودات متعاقبة بحيث إذا انتفى عن الماهية وجود في آن حصل لها في ذلك الآن بعينه وجود آخر أشد أو أزيد أو أضعف أو أنقص، وكذلك في الهيولى.\n\n**Translation**\n\nSomeone might object that the quiddity needs only *some* instance of existence, just as matter needs some instance of form. Successive existences might therefore come upon it: at the very instant one instance ceases, another stronger, greater, weaker or smaller instance obtains. Likewise successive forms might come upon matter.\n\n**Answer**\n\n> قلت وجوب بقاء الموضوع في الحركة بعينه يستدعي بقاء وجود بعينه مع الماهية بالضرورة، وإلا لكانت الماهية مع كل وجود فرداً آخر منها فلا تكون باقية بعينها.\n\nI reply: the requirement that the subject of motion remain numerically the same necessarily requires the persistence of **the same existence** belonging to the quiddity. Otherwise, with each instance of existence the quiddity would be a different instance of itself and would not persist numerically unchanged. The same applies to matter in relation to its form.\n\n**Core pressure:** identity is not guaranteed by the formal claim that “the same essence” is being predicated at each stage. Lāhījī thinks the identity of the *actual subject* is required for a continuous physical motion.\n\n### L5. The second argument: do infinitely many intermediate states exist actually or potentially?\n\nLāhījī presents a further response. In continuous motion the indefinitely many individual positions or qualitative stages traversed between two limits are not all individually actual as discrete, instantaneously occupied states. If a moving thing really occupied an actual determinate state at every supposed instant, either it would remain at one state long enough to interrupt motion, or a succession of actually existing indivisible instants would follow, which cannot form a continuous time.\n\nApplied to movement *in existence itself*, this analysis threatens to leave the quiddity actually nonexistent between posited stages, since its instances of existence would be merely potential.\n\n**Arabic (selected)**\n\n> فلو تحركت الماهية في الوجود لزم أن يكون جميع أفراد وجودها في زمان الحركة بالقوة فيلزم كون الماهية حال الحركة في الوجود موجودة بالقوة لا بالفعل.\n\n**Translation**\n\nIf a quiddity moved with respect to existence, all its instances of existence during the time of motion would have to be potential, and the quiddity during that motion would exist potentially rather than actually.\n\n### L6. Objection from motion in place, and answer\n\nAn interlocutor objects that the body cannot be deprived of an actual location. Lāhījī answers that what is needed is not its occupation of each discrete **terminal** location at each instant; the actuality of **being between** the extremes can account for motion while the individual points are potential divisions.\n\nA final objection proposes that an actual specific state exists at each hypothetically chosen instant, without successive instants: between any two posited instants lies time. Lāhījī replies that the quiddity would then lack actual existence during that intervening time if the relevant actual instances were restricted to each posited instant.\n\n**The unit closes with an instruction to reflect** (*fa-tadabbar*), not a full rejection of all concepts of continuous individual existence.\n\n### Critical evaluation of Lāhījī's premises\n\nHis case rests on a **subject-and-determination model of motion**:\n\n1. The moving subject exists actually.\n2. The determination in which it moves must be distinct from what makes that subject actual.\n3. A numerically identical subject persists during the reception of successive determinations.\n4. Existence and constitutive form cannot be treated as determinations that a subject already actual *without them* receives.\n5. Therefore movement in existence and in constitutive substance cannot occur in the same manner as movement in place or heat.\n\nThe vulnerable premise is **(2)**: must all continuity be analysed as an already actual subject bearing different ontologically derivative features? Ṣadrā answers **no**.\n\n---\n\n## III. Ṣadrā's specific rebuttal: the continuity belongs to existence (*Asfār* IV:85–87)\n\nThese passages are *direct source* for Ṣadrā, but they are **not** evidence that Ṣadrā is personally answering Lāhījī's exact wording. They are a philosophical juxtaposition of independently located arguments.\n\n### S1. One flowing individual can possess potentially infinite determinations\n\n**Arabic — IV:85**\n\n> فيمكن اشتداده واستكماله في ذاته بحيث يكون وجود واحد شخصي مستمر متفاوت الحصول في شخصيته ووحدته الجوهرية بحيث ينتزع منه معنى نوع آخر بالقوة في كل آن يفرض.\n\n**Translation**\n\nIt can therefore become more intense and reach further perfection in its own essence, while being **one continuous individual existence**, differing in its mode of obtaining within its individuality and substantial unity, such that a different specific determination could potentially be abstracted from it at each posited instant.\n\n**The difference:** Ṣadrā does not require innumerable separate **actual** substantial kinds at every instant. The differentiated substantial meanings are merely potential boundaries or analyses of a numerically continuous existence.\n\n### S2. The classic objection: either the species persists or a new species constantly replaces it\n\n**Arabic — IV:85**\n\n> أما أن يبقى نوعه في وسط الاشتداد مثلاً أو لا يبقى، فإن كان يبقى نوعه فما تغيرت الصورة الجوهرية في ذاتها ... وإن كان الجوهر لا يبقى مع الاشتداد مثلاً فكان الاشتداد قد أحدث جوهراً آخر ...\n\n**Translation**\n\nEither its species remains while it becomes more intense, or it does not. If it remains, its substantial form has not changed in itself, but only in an accident; if it does not remain, the intensification has produced another substance, and each posited instant would introduce a further new substance.\n\nṢadrā reports this as the objection of Ibn Sīnā and earlier philosophers, **not as his own conclusion**.\n\n### S3. Reply: continuity of one existence is not persistence of a fixed specific meaning\n\n**Arabic — IV:86**\n\n> إن أريد ببقائه وجوده بالشخص فنختار أنه باق ... لأن الوجود المتصل التدريجي الواحد أمر واحد زماني والاشتداد كمالية في ذلك الوجود.\n\n**Translation**\n\nIf by its persistence they mean that the **individual existence** persists, we accept that it does: one continuously developing existence is one temporal actuality, and intensification is a perfection belonging to that existence.\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> فلا محالة يتبدل عليه صفات ذاتية جوهرية ولم يلزم منه وجود أنواع بلا نهاية بالفعل، بل هناك وجود واحد شخصي متصل له حدود غير متناهية بالقوة.\n\n**Translation**\n\nEssential and substantial determinations inevitably change within it, but that does not entail an infinity of actual species. Rather, there is **one continuous individual existence** with infinitely many possible boundaries.\n\n**Editorial qualification:** The online page contains interleaved glosses and note markers (*s rah* etc.). Only Ṣadrā's running argument is translated; those glosses belong to a separate editorial stratum and have not been silently incorporated.\n\n### S4. Does movement need an enduring *substrate*?\n\n**Arabic — IV:87**\n\n> الأصل في كل شيء هو وجوده والماهية تبع له ... وموضوع كل حركة وإن وجب أن يكون باقياً بوجوده وتشخصه إلا أنه يكفي في تشخص الموضوع الجسماني أن يكون هناك مادة تتشخص بوجود صورة ما ...\n\n**Translation**\n\nThe fundamental reality of each thing is its existence, while its quiddity follows upon it. Although the subject of every movement must indeed persist in its existence and individuation, it suffices for a bodily subject's individuation that there be matter individualised through the presence of some form ...\n\nThis is a radical reformulation of what **persists**. Ṣadrā can preserve continuity while treating the continuous actual **existence** as the principle of identity, rather than taking an unchanged specific quiddity as a subject to which substantial states are attached.\n\n### What this rebuttal does and does not establish\n\n- **Established in Ṣadrā's own text:** a continuous individual existence can support substantial intensification without an actual infinity of simultaneously or successively separate species.\n- **Not yet established:** that every natural substance in ordinary physical science must be understood this way, or that the proposed continuity is demonstrated independently of Ṣadrā's account of existence.\n- **Key philosophical objection still live:** can **individual unity of existence** itself be specified non-circularly without presupposing the very continuity whose possibility is under dispute?\n\n---\n\n## IV. AT comparison: not all “substantial change” is the same\n\nAristotelian–Thomistic philosophy permits **substantial generation and corruption** and genuine powers of development within living substances. It ordinarily denies that one numerically identical substantial form is simply a continuous series of different substantial species in the same sense Ṣadrā proposes.\n\nBut the following are distinct:\n\n1. **Accidental alteration of a persistent substance** (a body becomes hotter).\n2. **Growth and maturation of one organism** (same natural kind, developing powers and accidents).\n3. **Substantial generation/corruption** (loss or acquisition of constitutive substantial form).\n4. **Ṣadrian substantial motion** (the existence of a material substance intrinsically flows toward different degrees of actuality).\n\nA comparison that calls (2) “substantial motion” because it involves real development would **equivocate**. A stronger comparison would ask whether the unity of a changing organism's life admits degrees of actuality that can be understood without converting a substance into a succession of numerically different substances.\n\n### The new philosophical synthesis to test\n\n**Proposition:** An individual may have real developmental continuity, with varying determinations of actuality, while retaining substantial identity.\n\n- Lāhījī supplies the **identity constraint**: changes presuppose a numerically identifiable subject.\n- Ṣadrā supplies the **continuity possibility**: numerical unity may belong to one flowing existence, not merely to an unchanging kind.\n- AT supplies an account of **act, potency, form and intrinsic powers**, including the distinction between what it is to retain the same substantial nature and what it is to undergo substantial generation.\n\nThere may be a useful new synthesis around **analogical grades of actuality within enduring substances**, but this is a **proposed philosophical development**, not a position to be attributed to Ibn Sīnā, Lāhījī or Aquinas without further texts.\n\n## V. What actually changed in the tradition?\n\n**A. From Ibn Sīnā and al-Ḥillī to Lāhījī:** the traditional claim that substantial form is not acquired through continuous motion is preserved, but Lāhījī develops an explicit argument from **persistence of the existence of the subject** and meets a specific objection about replacing instances of existence at the same instant. That is more than slogan-level conservatism.\n\n**B. Ṣadrā's real innovation:** the very individuation of a material thing can be its **continuous existential flow**, with different substantive determinations only potentially rather than actually distinct. The debate is therefore about individuation, existence and how the continuum is analysed, not merely about whether material beings change.\n\n**C. Later Peripatetic response:** a historian cannot infer that *every* late Avicennian rejects substantial motion. In particular, the nineteenth-century Jilwa is reported as the author of a printed *Ithbāt al-ḥaraka al-jawhariyya*, [Tehran 1313 AH print noted in al-Ṭihrānī's *al-Dharīʿa*](https://almntqim.com/%D9%A3%D9%A3%D9%A6%D9%A6_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B0%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%B9%D8%A9-%D8%A2%D9%82%D8%A7-%D8%A8%D8%B2%D8%B1%DA%AF-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B7%D9%87%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A-%D8%AC-%D9%A1/%D9%A8%D9%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B5%D9%81%D8%AD%D8%A9). Its **title and physical printing** are documented, but its thesis, precise argument and relationship to Sadrian substantial motion **must be inspected directly**. The previous reflexive classification “Jilwa = opponent of substantial motion” is retracted.\n\n### Apparatus requirements for a definitive critical edition\n\n- Collate the full Lāhījī sixth question with additional editions; particularly his distinction between *tashkīk* among instances and *motion* of one individual.\n- Retrieve the relevant parts of Avicenna's *Physics* and *Metaphysics*, and Rāzī's objections, rather than relying on Ṣadrā's report of their position.\n- Keep al-Ḥillī's **matn** and modern editor's footnotes separate.\n- Collate Ṣadrā's running text IV:85–87 with its glosses (e.g. Sabzawārī), separately attributed.\n- Locate and inspect the 1313 AH Jilwa print: *Ithbāt al-ḥaraka al-jawhariyya*, and distinguish it from other writings titled *Taḥqīq al-ḥaraka fī l-jawhar*.\n- Compare the exact Latin *De principiis naturae* and Aristotle's *Physics* V before declaring an AT consensus about the indivisibility of substantial generation.\n\n**Completion statement:** Lāhījī's sixth question is translated as a connected unit, with Arabic lemmata; Ṣadrā's principal reply is translated in selected connected portions; al-Ḥillī's two historical claims are translated from their loci. This is a substantive philosophical collation, **not a multi-manuscript critical edition** or proof of Jilwa's doctrinal allegiance.\n"},{"id":"ch04","kind":"analysis","author":"GNAMMA scholarly editorial synthesis","work":"Late Avicennian Summa · comparative chapters","title":"Intellect, soul and identity","discipline":"Psychology","locus":"Working chapter","lang":"mixed","family":"Comparative analysis","coverage":"Working analytical chapter with selected primary passages","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/gnamma-research/booksite/blob/master/research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/04-intellect-soul-and-identity.md","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/04-intellect-soul-and-identity.md","topics":["intellect","knowledge","universals"],"sourceCoverage":"Selected Arabic/Latin excerpts; no full primary witness","manuscriptCollated":false,"md":"# Book III, Chapter 1 — Intellect, abstraction, the soul and personal identity\n**Ibn Sīnā, *al-Nafs* V.5; al-Rāzī; Mullā Ṣadrā; Aquinas**\n\n**Textual status:** Six directly source-located translations from Ibn Sīnā's *al-Shifāʾ: al-Nafs*, volume/part V.5, reported printed pp. 321–337; two from Ṣadrā, *Asfār* V:190; selected Latin from Aquinas, *Summa theologiae* I q. 85 a. 2 and I qq. 75–76. This is **a connected, multi-source argumentative chapter**, not a continuous translation of the sixteen pages or a manuscript-critical edition. Secondary marginal notes in the Arabic edition and the Rāzī excerpt quoted by the editor are clearly distinguished from Ibn Sīnā's own text.\n\n**Sources:** [Ibn Sīnā, *al-Nafs*, V.5 (Rafed, searchable with original printed page markers)](https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=904&page=340&type=c_fbook); [Ṣadrā, *Asfār* V:190](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/1607/190); [Aquinas, *Summa theologiae* I qq. 84–85](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html); [Aquinas, I qq. 75–76](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html).\n\n## I. Why cognition requires a further actualising cause — Ibn Sīnā, *al-Nafs* V.5\n\n**Arabic — reported printed p. 321**\n\n> إن النفس الإنسانية قد تكون عاقلة بالقوة ثم تصير عاقلة بالفعل، وكل ما خرج من القوة إلى الفعل فإنما يخرج بسبب بالفعل يخرجه. فهاهنا سبب هو الذي يخرج نفوسنا في المعقولات من القوة إلى الفعل.\n\n**Translation**\n\nThe human soul may at one time be intellectual only potentially and subsequently become intellectual actually. Whatever passes from potency into act does so through a cause that is already actual and brings it forth. There must therefore be a cause that brings our souls from potential to actual understanding of intelligibles.\n\n**Argument structure:** the soul's developing knowledge requires an actualising cause. The immediate inference is to an actual cause of **intelligibility**. It does not itself prove a single separate universal agent: that identification requires further premises.\n\n**Arabic — continued**\n\n> وإذا هو السبب في إعطاء الصور العقلية فليس إلا عقلاً بالفعل عنده مبادئ الصور العقلية مجردة، ونسبته إلى نفوسنا كنسبة الشمس إلى إبصارنا.\n\n**Translation**\n\nSince it is the cause that bestows intelligible forms, it must be an intellect actually possessing the principles of those intelligible forms in an immaterial state. Its relation to our souls is like the relation of the sun to our vision.\n\nThe analogy is carefully restricted: the sun enables sight without being identical either to the eye or to the colours seen, and the Actual Intellect makes intellectual apprehension possible without being identical to the individual human intellect.\n\n## II. How sensory imagination prepares the soul\n\n**Arabic — Ibn Sīnā, reported printed p. 322**\n\n> لا على أنها نفسها تنتقل من التخيل إلى العقل منا ... بل على معنى أن مطالعتها تعد النفس لأن يفيض عليها المجرد من العقل الفعال.\n\n**Translation**\n\nThe imagined particulars do not themselves move from imagination into our intellect. Rather, attending to them prepares the soul so that the immaterial intelligible can be bestowed upon it by the Active Intellect.\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> فإن الأفكار والتأملات حركات معدة للنفس نحو قبول الفيض، كما أن الحدود الوسطى معدة بنحو أشد تأكيداً لقبول النتيجة.\n\n**Translation**\n\nOur acts of thought and reflection are preparatory movements disposing the soul to receive the emanation, just as middle terms [in demonstration] dispose it still more decisively to receive the conclusion.\n\n**Important distinction:** Sensible experience is **not pointless** in this account. Its role is genuinely preparatory, and disciplined demonstration determines which content can be received. The division of epistemic labour, however, differs significantly from a theory in which the human agent intellect itself abstracts the universal intelligible form from phantasms.\n\n### A cross-domain result\n\nThe **epistemic** role of the Active Intellect has to be distinguished from its **cosmological** role as Giver of Forms. Ibn Sīnā employs related language of preparation and receiving forms in both, but neither role can be automatically inferred from the other when reconstructing **later** philosophers. A nineteenth-century thinker could revise the one without clearly revising the other.\n\n## III. Why learning is not a permanent storehouse of fully actual thoughts\n\nIbn Sīnā's *Nafs* V.5 considers whether a learned intelligible that is not currently being thought remains present in a continuously actual, fully cognised condition, or is recovered through stored bodily representations, or depends on renewed intellectual actualisation.\n\n**Arabic — reported printed p. 336**\n\n> فبقي أن يكون القسم الصحيح هو القسم الأخير، ويكون التعلم طلب الاستعداد التام للاتصال به ... فتفيض منه الصور مفصلة في النفس بتوسط الفكرة.\n\n**Translation**\n\nThe remaining correct alternative is the last: learning is the acquisition of a complete preparedness for connection with [the Active Intellect] ... so that detailed intelligible forms are bestowed from it upon the soul through the mediation of thinking.\n\n**Arabic — reported printed p. 337**\n\n> وإذا قيل إن فلاناً عالم بالمعقولات فمعناه أنه بحيث كلما شاء أحضر صورته في ذهن نفسه ... ليس أن ذلك المعقول حاضر في ذهنه ومتصور في عقله بالفعل دائماً.\n\n**Translation**\n\nWhen someone is called knowledgeable about intelligibles, this means that the person is so disposed that, whenever he wills, he can bring the intelligible form before his mind; it does not mean that the intelligible remains present in his mind, actually understood at all times.\n\n**Critical implication:** An acquired **habit or stable disposition** (*malaka*) is distinct from the current **act** of considering an intelligible. Ibn Sīnā's explanation introduces an external intellectual source into this transition. A Thomistic explanation can preserve the habit/act distinction without adopting the same separate mediator.\n\nThis is relevant to a wider theory of acquired intellectual dispositions, teaching, and educational formation: a student's possessing a science is not equivalent to simultaneously contemplating every proposition in it.\n\n## IV. The real disagreement over the union of knower and known\n\nIn Ibn Sīnā's psychology, becoming an actual knower of a thing does not, for our individual human soul, automatically make the knower and known **numerically one substance**.\n\n**Arabic — *Nafs* V.5, reported printed p. 329 (selective)**\n\n> فلا يكون العقل والعاقل والمعقول شيئاً واحداً في أنفسنا، نعم هذا في شيء آخر يمكن أن يكون على ما سنلمحه في موضعه.\n\n**Translation**\n\nTherefore intellect, the one who understands, and what is understood are not one and the same thing *in our case*. This could, however, be true of something else, as we shall indicate in the appropriate place.\n\n**Editor's separately quoted Rāzī argument, NOT Ibn Sīnā's own prose:** In the modern edition, a note quotes Rāzī's *al-Mabāḥith al-mashriqiyya*: if the knower literally becomes the first intelligible and subsequently a different intelligible, it is unclear how the original knowledge can persist without one individual becoming two different essences. The original *Mabāḥith* passage needs its own collation.\n\n**Sadrian contrast:** Ṣadrā develops a much stronger role for unity of the intellect, knower, and intelligible. It is essential to specify **which level** of intellectual knowing is claimed to involve existential unity, rather than parody his doctrine as though it were ordinary physical identity between two objects.\n\n## V. Ṣadrā on the identity of a human being through bodily change — *Asfār* V:190\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> الأصل السابع أن هوية البدن وتشخصه إنما يكونان بنفسه لا بجرمه، فزيد مثلاً زيد بنفسه لا بجسده، ولأجل ذلك يستمر وجوده وتشخصه ما دامت النفس باقية فيه وإن تبدلت أجزاؤه.\n\n**Translation**\n\nSeventh principle: the identity and individuation of the body belong to it through its soul, not through its bulk of matter. Thus Zayd is Zayd through his soul, not through his body; and therefore his being and individuation continue so long as his soul remains within it, even if the body's material components are replaced.\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> فإن الهوية الإنسانية في جميع هذه التحولات والتقلبات واحدة هي هي بعينها لأنها واقعة على سبيل الاتصال الوحداني التدريجي.\n\n**Translation**\n\nFor human identity through all these transformations and changes is one and numerically the same, since they occur through a gradual continuity that possesses unity.\n\n**Important qualification:** This is not an attribution of unchanging substantial form throughout every phase of the person's development. Ṣadrā's framework makes personal identity involve **continuous development in existence**, including the claim that the human soul is corporeal in origination and spiritual in persistence. The exact argument for that further doctrine requires additional passages from the *Asfār*.\n\n### An apparent puzzle with the subject of substantial motion\n\n- A material body changes in parts, states, faculties and (according to Ṣadrā) intrinsic existence.\n- The soul constitutes the human's central principle of identity.\n- This gives a stronger account of **personal unity through transformation**, but it also raises a question: does the soul itself possess a continuous identity independently of an already unified temporal life, or is that unity constituted by the existential course?\n\nThe same identity question arose in [Lāhījī's objection to substantial motion](03-substantial-motion-dispute.md). Thus the psychology and natural philosophy must be analysed together.\n\n## VI. Aquinas on what the intellect knows\n\n**Latin — *ST* I q. 85 a. 2**\n\n> *Species intelligibilis non est quod intelligitur actu, sed id quo intelligit intellectus.*\n\n**Translation**\n\nThe intelligible species is not what is actually understood; it is what **enables** the intellect to understand.\n\nIn the body of the article Aquinas argues that if all sciences concerned merely the species internal to the knower, natural sciences would not be about external things. Intellect is thereby capable of knowing **the nature in the thing**, while its act of understanding belongs to the knower.\n\nAquinas also explains abstraction from phantasms and the agent intellect's activity in *ST* I qq. 84–85, rejecting the claim that all intelligibles necessarily come into us from separately existing Forms.\n\n### The genuinely difficult comparison\n\n| Question | Ibn Sīnā | Aquinas | Ṣadrā |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Human rational knowing starts in potentiality | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Imagery and sensation can prepare intellectual thought | Yes | Yes | Yes, within a different epistemological synthesis |\n| A separate Active Intellect confers intelligible forms | Yes, in the classical account | Not as the universal immediate producer of all intelligibles | Requires differentiation across levels of intellect and existential union |\n| Knowledge as a habit differs from current intellection | Yes | Yes | Yes; exact theory requires collation |\n| Human understanding entails identity of human subject and object in the same strong sense | Not in the ordinary individual case | No: the species is *quo*, nature is *quod* | Stronger existential unity thesis |\n| Personal bodily identity depends on substantial form/soul | Yes, broadly | Yes, in hylomorphic terms | Yes, but as a continuous existential development |\n\nThe **first two agreements are real** but do not erase the major difference over the separate intellect as a source of universal intelligibles. Nor does the difference negate the common Aristotelian starting point of potency, act, intellectual form and knowledge of real natures.\n\n## VII. Two especially useful implications for the AT synthesis\n\n### A. Don't mistake agency of the human intellect for the cosmological Giver of Forms\n\nNatural formation of a new substance and abstraction of an intelligible nature are two distinct causal operations. In Ibn Sīnā's scheme the Active Intellect figures in both, but their separation matters:\n\n1. Could the **separate bestowal of natural forms** be rejected while preserving a form of intellectual illumination?\n2. Could the **human agent intellect** perform abstraction without thereby being the sufficient efficient cause of intellectual subsistence?\n3. Could the causation of substantial forms be both natural and dependent upon God without adding the separate tenth intelligence as an ontologically necessary mediator?\n\nA coherent synthesis has to decide these separately, not conflate them under the vague phrase “vertical causality.”\n\n### B. A shared doctrine of substantial personal unity need not imply an identical theory of change\n\nAquinas and Ibn Sīnā can agree that a person is not merely a heap of material parts. Ṣadrā radicalises the continuity question by applying it to the substance's very becoming. The philosophical issue is whether the same-person relation is best understood through persistent form, continuous existence, or a carefully differentiated combination.\n\n## VIII. Important limits\n\nThe Arabic edition of *al-Nafs* available through Rafed contains **modern marginal notes and quotations** (including Ṣadrā and al-Rāzī) interleaved with Ibn Sīnā's text. Only passages directly attributed above to Ibn Sīnā are treated as authorial. The quoted Rāzī argument is marked an editorial citation. Different print page numbers from the web navigation have been identified by the **printed page labels within the transcription**.\n\nThis chapter contributes genuinely connected psychology, epistemology, substantial identity and Thomistic comparison; **it is not** the complete Arabic *Nafs* V.5 translation, and the broader Sadrian and Narāqian psychology still needs the required primary-source collation.\n"},{"id":"ch05","kind":"analysis","author":"GNAMMA scholarly editorial synthesis","work":"Late Avicennian Summa · comparative chapters","title":"Four causes and form-bestowal","discipline":"Natural philosophy","locus":"Working chapter","lang":"mixed","family":"Comparative analysis","coverage":"Working analytical chapter with selected primary passages","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/gnamma-research/booksite/blob/master/research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/05-four-causes-form-bestowal.md","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/05-four-causes-form-bestowal.md","topics":["forms","natural-generation","causation"],"sourceCoverage":"Selected Arabic/Latin excerpts; no full primary witness","manuscriptCollated":false,"md":"# Book II, Chapter 2 — The four causes and the Giver of Forms\n**A primary-text confrontation between Ibn Sīnā's natural and metaphysical causes and Aquinas's account of substantial generation**\n\n**Textual status.** The complete *four causes* definition unit on *Shifāʾ: Ilāhiyyāt* VI.1, printed p. 257, is translated below with the Arabic base and some actual published edition variants. The key account of disposition and form-bestowal from IX.5, printed p. 413, is given in selective connected extracts with the Arabic. Aquinas's **specific reply to Avicenna**, *De potentia* III.8, is translated in a continuous selected argument comprising the stated opponent, his objection, Aquinas's positive account and the rational-soul exception. This is **a collation of printed texts and printed variants**, not a fresh inspection of manuscripts, and not a complete translation of both book chapters.\n\n**Source witnesses:**\n- Ibn Sīnā, *Ilāhiyyāt al-Shifāʾ* [VI.1, p.257](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/257) and [VI.4, p.278](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/278); [IX.5, p.413](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/413).\n- Same Avicennian work [II.4 p.120](https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B4%D9%81%D8%A7%D8%A1-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%87%D9%8A%D8%A7%D8%AA-2/v/1/p/120), [II.4 p.121](https://najafdesertlibrary.com/book/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B4%D9%81%D8%A7%D8%A1-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%87%D9%8A%D8%A7%D8%AA-2/v/1/p/121).\n- Thomas Aquinas, [*De potentia* III.8, corpus and ad 7–8](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/qdp3.html), Corpus Thomisticum [58809] and [58816–58817].\n- **Editorial distinction:** printed variant sigla in the *Shifāʾ* transcription are reproduced only when explicitly recorded. No full descriptions or dates are inferred for the sigla without the edition's prefatory key.\n\n## 1. Avicenna defines the four causes, and the *two uses* of “efficient”\n\n### Arabic — VI.1, printed p.257, from «قد تكلمنا» through the definition of the end\n\n> قد تكلمنا في أمر الجواهر والأعراض، وفي اعتبار التقدم والتأخر فيها، وفي معرفة مطابقة الحدود للمحدودات الكلية والجزئية. فبالحري أن نتكلم الآن في العلة والمعلول، فإنهما أيضا من اللواحق التي تلحق الموجود بما هو موجود.\n>\n> والعلل كما سمعت، صورة وعنصر وفاعل وغاية.\n>\n> فنقول: إنا نعني بالعلة الصورية، العلة التي هي جزء من قوام الشيء، يكون الشيء بها هو ما هو بالفعل، وبالعنصرية العلة التي هي جزء من قوام الشيء، يكون بها الشيء هو ما هو بالقوة، وتستقر فيها قوة وجوده، وبالفاعل، العلة التي تفيد وجودا مباينا لذاتها، أي لا تكون ذاتها بالقصد الأول محلا لما يستفيد منها وجود شيء يتصور بها، حتى يكون في ذاتها قوة وجوده إلا بالعرض، ومع ذلك فيجب ألا يكون ذلك الوجود من أجله من جهة ما هو فاعل، بل إن كان ولا بد فباعتبار آخر، وذلك لأن الفلاسفة الإلهيين ليسوا يعنون بالفاعل مبدأ التحريك فقط، كما يعنيه الطبيعيون، بل مبدأ الوجود ومفيده، مثل الباري للعالم، وأما العلة الفاعلية الطبيعية فلا تفيد وجودا غير التحريك بأحد أنحاء التحريكات، فيكون مفيد الوجود في الطبيعيات مبدأ حركة، ونعني بالغاية، العلة التي لأجلها يحصل وجود شيء مباين لها.\n\n### English — connected translation of the complete definitions unit\n\nWe have discussed substances and accidents, how priority and posteriority are considered in them, and how definitions correspond to the universal and particular things defined. It is therefore appropriate now to speak about **cause and effect**, since these too are properties that belong to the existent insofar as it is existent.\n\nThe causes, as you have heard, are **form, element [matter], agent and end**.\n\nWe mean by the **formal cause** that cause which is a constitutive part of a thing and through which the thing is actually what it is. By the **material or elemental cause** we mean the constitutive part of a thing through which the thing is potentially what it is, and in which the potentiality for its existence resides.\n\nBy the **agent** we mean a cause which bestows an existence distinct from its own essence. That is, the agent's own essence is not, primarily and as such, the subject in which the existence of what receives existence from it is realised; it is not in itself the potency of that existence except incidentally. Furthermore, the existence conferred must not be something for the sake of which the agent acts *qua* agent; if it is an end at all, it is so under another description.\n\nThis is because **metaphysical philosophers do not mean by an agent merely a principle that causes motion**, as natural philosophers do. They mean a principle and bestower of **existence**, as in the case of the Creator's relation to the cosmos. A **natural efficient cause**, on the other hand, does not bestow an existence other than movement of one of its kinds. Thus the source of existence in natural philosophy is a principle of movement.\n\nFinally, by the **end** we mean the cause for whose sake the existence of something other than that end is brought about.\n\n### Critical apparatus to the primary source\n\nThis is a remarkably explicit statement and should govern, not merely decorate, the AT comparison: Avicenna himself distinguishes an *efficient cause of motion in the natural sciences* from an *efficient cause bestowing existence in metaphysics*. The texts do not simply assert “causality is vertical” or “natural agents are unreal.”\n\n**Printed variants, VI.1 p.257** (edition sigla are exactly those printed in the online transcription; spelling and punctuation otherwise normalised):\n\n| Locus | Running reading | Reported alternative | Source-critical implication |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| «فإنهما أيضا من اللواحق» preceding prose | «الجواهر» | «الجوهر» in ب | Singular/plural distinction, does not affect causal definition |\n| «فبالحري أن نتكلم الآن في العلة» | «في العلة» | «في العلية» in ح، ص، ط | Whether discourse is about *cause* or *causality*; local terminology shifts |\n| «والعلل كما سمعت» | «سمعت» | «سمعته» in ب، ص، ط، م | Grammatical, no doctrinal change |\n| «فنقول إنا نعني» | «فنقول» | «والعلل» in ح، د، ص، م | Different transition framing |\n| «العلة التي هي جزء من قوام الشيء» (material) | phrase present | omitted in م | A substantive compression of the material cause's constitutive status |\n| «يفيد وجوداً مبايناً لذاتها» | «لذاتها» | «لذاته» in ح | Antecedent/reference changes |\n| «من جهة ما هو فاعل» | «من جهة» | «من جهته» in ب، د، ط | Small syntactic difference |\n\n**Edition caveat:** These are **reported printed editorial variants**, not the results of examining the manuscript witnesses labelled ب، ح، ص، ط etc. Do not pretend to have consulted those manuscripts.\n\n## 2. Intrinsic form and material potentiality: no reified substrate\n\nIn *Ilāhiyyāt* II.4 Ibn Sīnā explains that corporeal matter becomes actual **only in the presence of form**. Matter's receptivity and the form's actualisation are not two complete substances that are independently actual and then combined. The argument, from II.4 p.120, begins:\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> فقد صح أن المادة الجسمانية إنما تقوم بالفعل عند وجود الصورة وأيضا فإن الصورة المادية ليست توجد مفارقة للمادة.\n\n**Translation**\n\nIt has therefore been established that corporeal matter is actual only upon the presence of form; likewise, material form does not exist separately from matter.\n\nThe following discussion distinguishes a genuine causal order of ontological dependence from two things that merely correlate in existence: the negation of one may accompany the negation of the other without **causing** that negation. This anticipates the later Lāhījī concern: a changing subject cannot simply be defined as matter already actual **apart from** its substantial form.\n\n**Evidence boundary:** Only the opening proposition and the following page's essential philosophical distinction have been checked; the entire II.4 chapter is not translated here.\n\n## 3. From cosmic disposition to the *wāhib al-ṣuwar*\n\nThe cosmological context of *Ilāhiyyāt* IX.5 matters. Ibn Sīnā first considers a proposed derivation of elemental qualities from celestial rotation—heating near the region of fire, cooling near earth, differing degrees of condensation and rarefaction. He criticises the proposal as insufficiently demonstrative and suggests the actual order may be more complex.\n\n### Arabic — IX.5, p.413, selective connected passage\n\n> وأن تكون هذه المادة التي تحدث بالشركة يفيض إليها من الأجرام السماوية، إما عن أربعة أجرام، وإما عن عدة منحصرة في أربع جمل، عن كل واحد منها ما يتهيأ لصورة جسم بسيط.\n>\n> فإذا استعد نال الصورة من واهب الصور، أو يكون ذلك كله يفيض عن جرم واحد، وأن يكون هناك سبب يوجب انقساما من الأسباب الخفية علينا.\n\n### Translation\n\nThe matter that thus comes to be through their combined action may receive an emanation from celestial bodies—either from four bodies, or from a number arranged in four groups—from each of which it receives what **prepares it** for the form of a simple body.\n\n**When it is suitably disposed, it receives the form from the Giver of Forms.** Alternatively, all of this might flow from one celestial body, with some cause hidden from us accounting for its differentiated effects.\n\nThis is a direct **primary-source statement**, not merely the modern historian's reconstruction, of the difference between *becoming disposed* (*istaʿadda*) and *receiving the form* from the *wāhib al-ṣuwar*.\n\n### Ibn Sīnā immediately criticises a contrary elemental-genesis model\n\n**Arabic — IX.5, p.413**\n\n> فإنك إن أردت أن تعرف ضعف ما قالوه، فتأمل أنهم يوجبون أن يكون الوجود أولا للجسم، وليس له في نفسه إحدى الصور المقومة غير الصورة الجسمية، وإنما يكتسب سائر الصور بالحركة والسكون ثانيا ... وبينا أن الجسم لا يستكمل له وجود بمجرد الصورة الجسمية، ما لم تقترن بها صورة أخرى.\n\n**Translation**\n\nIf you wish to recognise the weakness of their account, consider that they suppose a body first exists without any constitutive form beyond corporeal form, and acquires its other forms only subsequently through motion and rest. But we have already explained that a body is not complete in its being through corporeal form alone unless another form is joined to it.\n\n**Interpretive constraint:** The text is about the **origination of the elements**, not in isolation a proof that *every* naturally generated organism's complete form must be attributed in precisely the same way to the Giver. The stronger universal claim requires the *Kawn wa-fasād* and natural-philosophical texts.\n\n### A real, documented printed variant matters\n\nThe running reading on p.413 is «فإذا استعد نال الصورة من واهب الصور». The editor's apparatus reports one alternative reading for «استعد نال» (marked ه) and reports «الصور» instead of «الصورة» in some witnesses (د، م). The former affects the verbal phrase, the latter the singular/plural of the received form. This is **edition-level variant information**, not a fresh physical-manuscript collation. We use the printed running text in the translation and retain the recorded alternatives here.\n\n## 4. Aquinas' explicit reconstruction of Avicenna and objection to it\n\n**Latin — *De potentia* III.8, corpus, [58809] (selected contiguous argument)**\n\n> Et quia operatio naturae non potest esse ex nihilo, et per consequens oportet quod sit ex praesuppositione, non operabatur, secundum eos, natura, nisi ex parte materiae disponendo ipsam ad formam.\n>\n> Formam vero, quam oportet fieri et non praesupponi, oportet esse ex agente qui non praesupponit aliquid, sed potest ex nihilo facere ... Et hoc Avicenna dixit esse intelligentiam ultimam inter substantias separatas.\n\n**Translation**\n\nBecause nature cannot act from nothing, and consequently must act from something already presupposed, some held that nature works only upon the side of matter, **disposing it for form**.\n\nBut form itself, which had to be brought about rather than presupposed, they thought must come from an agent that presupposes nothing and can act from nothing. Avicenna identified that agent as **the last intelligence among the separate substances**.\n\n**Critical note:** This is Aquinas' report and criticism of the view, not a direct quotation from Ibn Sīnā. But Ibn Sīnā's own IX.5 passage above independently confirms the distinct role of the Giver of Forms in his account of the elements.\n\n### Aquinas' central objection: why would the natural generator have precisely that form?\n\n**Latin — *De potentia* III.8**\n\n> Quia cum unumquodque natum sit simile sibi agere ... non requireretur similitudo secundum formam substantialem in agente naturali, nisi forma substantialis geniti esset per actionem agentis ... inconveniens videtur, hoc generante praetermisso, aliud exterius inquirere.\n\n**Translation**\n\nSince it is natural for each thing to produce something similar to itself, a natural agent would not need to resemble its effect in **substantial form** unless the generated thing's substantial form actually resulted from the natural agent's activity. Since what must be acquired in the generated thing is already actual in the natural generator, it seems inappropriate to set that generator aside and seek some other external agent.\n\n**The force of the argument:** Natural agents are not mere preparatory triggers with no specific causal relation to the essential character of the generated thing. The match between the generator's substantial form and that of what it generates constitutes evidence of true proximate efficient causality.\n\n### Aquinas' positive resolution: it is the *composite*, not an isolated form, that comes to be\n\n**Latin — corpus**\n\n> Unumquodque autem factum, hoc modo dicitur fieri quo dicitur esse ... unde illud quod proprie fit per se, compositum est. Forma autem non proprie fit ... Nihil ergo obstat ... quin formas substantiales ex operatione naturae esse dicamus. Nam id quod fit, non est forma, sed compositum; quod ex materia fit, et non ex nihilo.\n>\n> ... Et sic non proprie dicitur quod forma fiat in materia, sed magis quod de materiae potentia educatur.\n\n**Translation**\n\nSomething is said to be **made** in the manner in which it is said to **exist**, since being is the terminus of production. Therefore what is properly and primarily produced is the **composite**. Form is not, properly speaking, itself made in isolation. There is thus no obstacle, on the ground that nature produces nothing from nothing, to affirming that substantial forms arise through the operation of nature: **what comes to be is not form considered alone, but the composite**, which comes from matter and not from nothing.\n\nAccordingly, we should not strictly say that form is independently produced *inside* matter; rather, it is **drawn forth from matter's potency** (*de materiae potentia educatur*).\n\nThis is the decisive explanatory reallocation: the form is intrinsic **in both thinkers**, but Aquinas makes the natural generator causally sufficient **in its own dependent order** to generate a composite, without treating the form as a separately created entity.\n\n### Exception: the human rational soul\n\n**Latin — *De potentia* III.8 ad 7**\n\n> licet anima rationalis habeat materiam in qua sit, non tamen educitur de potentia materiae, cum eius natura supra omnem materialem ordinem elevetur ...\n\n**Translation**\n\nAlthough the rational soul has matter **in which** it is present, it is not educed from matter's potency, for its nature is raised above every material order.\n\nThe rational soul is an explicit exception. Any synthesis saying “Aquinas thinks all forms simply come from natural material generators” is false.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Five causal levels that must remain differentiated\n\n| Level | Ibn Sīnā: secured by the passages here | Aquinas: secured by III.8 | A real agreement? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Formal constituent | Intrinsic substantial form actualises bodily matter | Intrinsic form actualises matter | **Yes**, in general hylomorphic architecture |\n| Material potentiality | Matter possesses potency and cannot be independently actual without form | Matter in potency to the composite/form | **Broadly** |\n| Natural efficient disposition | Celestial and natural factors dispose matter | Natural agent also disposes the material recipient | **Yes**, but not exhaustive |\n| Proximate substantial generation | The prepared element receives form from the Giver of Forms | The natural generator produces the composite; form educed from matter | **Not identical**; real explanatory disagreement |\n| Existential conservation | Metaphysical agent bestows existential actuality | Universal divine causation sustains every creature | **Shared dependency**, differences about cosmic mediation and divine freedom |\n\n### Strongest compatible reformulation — *explicitly proposed, not attributed*\n\nCould the Active Intellect be regarded as a **secondary but higher-order completing cause** of natural generation while natural generators are genuinely productive in their own right? Such an account would avoid two extremes—eliminating natural powers or making a separate intelligence causally unnecessary—but it would remain subject to Aquinas' objection: if the natural generator is sufficient in its own order for the composite, **what irreducible causal work remains for that particular separate intellect** rather than simply for divine universal concurrence?\n\nThe answer cannot be supplied by the observation that God creates and sustains all things. That would **change the question** from the role of the **created Active Intellect** to the role of the **uncreated First Cause**.\n\n## 6. The connections to later Avicennian revisions\n\n- **Lāhījī, *Shawāriq* I:159–161**: what an efficient cause makes is **particular existence**, and one actual concrete reality may be distinguished conceptually into quiddity and existence. This helps avoid reifying the form and actuality as two independent made items. [Chapter on *jaʿl*](02-making-and-causal-explanation.md).\n- **Ṣadrā, *Asfār* IV:85–87**: the very identity of a natural thing is a continuously developing individual existence. This potentially transforms how “receiving form” and “persists through change” are analysed, even without a demonstrated rejection of a hierarchy of intelligences. [Chapter on substantial motion](03-substantial-motion-dispute.md).\n- **Ibn Sīnā, *al-Nafs* V.5**: the separate Active Intellect also **actualises intellectual apprehension**. This epistemological role must not be silently treated as evidence for a later thinker's acceptance of its cosmological role. [Chapter on cognition](04-intellect-soul-and-identity.md).\n- **Narāqī, *Jāmiʿ al-afkār* I:38–42**: continuing existential dependence upon a cause is **not** the same as the efficient generation of new form; the builder objection operates at precisely this explanatory interface. [Working Narāqī dossier](../primary-translation-causation.md).\n- **Jilwa and Khayrābādī**: the collected bibliographies do not yet provide sufficient collated natural-philosophical Arabic to state conclusively whether either abolished, retained or transformed the Giver of Forms. Do not infer that from a school label.\n\n## 7. What this comparison actually establishes\n\n**Established from primary texts:** Avicenna explicitly distinguishes metaphysical and natural efficient causation, recognises matter and form as constitutive causes, and describes elemental matter as disposed for form received from the Giver. Aquinas explicitly identifies Avicenna's last separated intellect as the giver, rejects it as necessary to ordinary natural generation, and argues that the generated composite is properly the product of a natural generator.\n\n**Still contested:** whether a suitably refined account of **different orders of causal explanation** can reconcile their systems without losing Aquinas' real criticism. That requires examination of both Avicennian natural agency and the explanatory work of the last intelligence, plus subsequent developments.\n\n**Missing from a manuscript-critical edition:** complete *Shifāʾ* natural-philosophy texts on all species of generation; independent late author's direct treatment of *wāhib al-ṣuwar*; original printed witness examination and sigla key; matching Rāzian/Tūsian exchanges; all the edition variants. The small edition apparatus above is accurately confined to the **variants actually reported in the published source**, not falsely represented as fresh codex collation.\n"},{"id":"ch06","kind":"analysis","author":"GNAMMA scholarly editorial synthesis","work":"Late Avicennian Summa · comparative chapters","title":"Divine agency and plurality","discipline":"Theology","locus":"Working chapter","lang":"mixed","family":"Comparative analysis","coverage":"Working analytical chapter with selected primary passages","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/gnamma-research/booksite/blob/master/research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/06-divine-agency-necessity-and-plurality.md","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/06-divine-agency-necessity-and-plurality.md","topics":["divine-will","cosmology","modality"],"sourceCoverage":"Selected Arabic/Latin excerpts; no full primary witness","manuscriptCollated":false,"md":"# Book IV, Chapter 1 — The First's agency: necessity, will, mediation and cosmic plurality\n**Avicenna's divine simplicity and emanation confronted with Aquinas's divine agency, freedom and multiple effects**\n\n**Edition status:** Source-located direct Arabic/Latin comparative translation of (a) *Ilāhiyyāt al-Shifāʾ* IX.1 p.373 and IX.4 p.402, and (b) Aquinas, *De potentia* III.16. Printed edition variants from the *Shifāʾ* are identified where relevant; no manuscript witness was inspected independently. Not a continuous complete translation of either IX.1 or IX.4, nor of Aquinas's 16th article. Lāhījī and Narāqī are included only where their already-translated argument informs the synthesis, not falsely as direct participants in this particular dispute.\n\n**Primary witnesses:** [Ibn Sīnā, *Ilāhiyyāt* IX.1 p.373](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/373), [IX.4 p.402](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/402); [Thomas Aquinas, *De potentia* III.16, corpus](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/qdp3.html), especially textual markers [59134] and [59135].\n\n## I. Two arguments that must not be conflated\n\n**A. Modal thesis:** If a complete cause is actually in place and lacks no condition, its effect follows; a possible effect is necessary **through another** while remaining possible in itself. Lāhījī's *Shawāriq* I:88 and Narāqī's *Jāmiʿ* I:38–42 help refine this distinction.\n\n**B. Divine-agency thesis:** Whether the First **necessarily** determines this very cosmos and its intermediary intellects, or produces one of several possible cosmic orders through knowing and willing, is **not decided** by (A). Otherwise the causal modal proposition is being made to carry an additional cosmological and theological claim.\n\nAquinas's *De potentia* III.16 criticises Avicenna principally at the second level, not by denying that effects of actually complete causes have intelligible explanations.\n\n## II. Avicenna: divine simplicity and the permanent effect\n\n**Arabic — *Ilāhiyyāt* IX.1, printed p.373, selected connected text**\n\n> فقد ظهر لنا أن للكل مبدأ واجب الوجود، غير داخل في جنس أو واقع تحت حد أو برهان، بريء عن الكم والكيف والماهية والأين والمتى والحركة، لا ند له ولا شريك له ولا ضد له، وأنه واحد من جميع الوجوه ... وقد بان لك بعد ذلك أن واجب الوجود بذاته واجب الوجود من جميع جهاته، وأنه لا يجوز أن تستأنف له حالة لم تكن، مع أنه قد بان لك أن العلة لذاتها تكون موجبة المعلول، فإن دامت أوجبت المعلول دائماً.\n\n**Translation**\n\nIt has become clear that the whole has a principle that is **necessary in existence**, which falls under no genus, is subject to no definition or demonstration [of its essence], and is free of quantity, quality, quiddity, place, time and motion. It has neither equal nor partner nor opposite; it is one in every respect ...\n\nIt has further become clear that the Necessary Existent through itself is necessary in every respect and cannot begin to have a state that it previously lacked; and it has been established that a cause through its very essence **necessitates its effect**—if the cause endures, it necessitates its effect continually.\n\n**Critical note.** This connects divine immutability with the thesis of the complete cause's effect. It should not be dismissed as the assertion “God is a natural mechanism”; Ibn Sīnā also insists on divine knowing. But it is a genuine modal commitment whose implications for cosmic origination differ from those of the Thomistic freedom thesis.\n\n### Printed variants on p.373\n\n- In the lemma «في صفة فاعلية المبدأ الأول», the phrase «فاعلية» is reported as «ما عليه» in ب، ح، د. This changes the transmitted title or description and is editorially significant, though not conclusive about doctrine.\n- In «فإن دامت أوجبت المعلول دائما», the clause is found in the printed base reading; no substantive variant is reported there in the displayed apparatus.\n\n**Editorial boundary:** The ellipsis marks intervening claims about the First's unity and the first mover. The whole of IX.1 has not been reproduced.\n\n## III. Avicenna: neither purposive choice like ours nor blind nature\n\n**Arabic — IX.4, p.402**\n\n> قد صح لنا فيما قدمناه من القول أن الواجب الوجود بذاته واحد، وأنه ليس بجسم ولا في جسم ولا منقسم بوجه من الوجوه، فإذن الموجودات كلها وجودها عنه ... فلهذا لا يجوز أن يكون كون الكل عنه على سبيل قصد منه كقصدنا لتكوين الكل ولوجود الكل فيكون قاصدا لأجل شيء غيره ... وليس كون الكل عنه على سبيل الطبع بأن يكون وجود الكل عنه لا بمعرفة ولا لرضى منه، وكيف يصح هذا وهو عقل محض يعقل ذاته؟\n\n**Translation**\n\nIt has been established that the Necessary Existent through itself is one, neither a body nor located in a body, and in no respect divisible. It follows that the being of all existents is from it. It cannot be the cause of their existence in the manner of **deliberation like our own**, intending the production of the cosmos for the sake of something other than itself. Yet the whole does **not** proceed from it as a **blind natural process**, without knowledge or satisfaction: how could that be, when the First is pure intellect understanding itself?\n\n### Why this is a more difficult position than the textbook formula suggests\n\nIbn Sīnā excludes two **different** inadequate accounts:\n\n1. **Human purposive deliberation** that seeks an additional benefit outside the perfect First; such benefit would imply something the First lacked.\n2. **Unknowing natural production** that would contradict the First's pure intellectual self-knowledge.\n\nThe First's agency proceeds through intellectual understanding without a temporal transition into intending or realising a new purpose. One cannot responsibly say “Avicenna's First has no knowledge of effects,” because the quoted text expressly rejects unknowing operation. Equally, one must not assume that “the First knows what proceeds” establishes *libertarian alternative possibilities* in the Thomistic sense.\n\n**Printed variant warning:** The same passage reports “لا بمعرفة” versus a variant “لمعرفة” in د. Such a change alters the local positive/negative wording, and cannot be ignored when discussing knowledgeable production. The running text contrasts action “without knowledge” with the First's *actual* intellect; the surrounding argumentative grammar governs the reading. A further variant «ولا لرضى» / «رضى» is recorded. The text should be checked against the edition's witness key before privileging one interpretation of divine approval.\n\n## IV. Aquinas's reconstruction and criticism: the First's simplicity does not impose a single first effect\n\n**Latin — *De potentia* III.16**\n\n> Alii pluralitatem rerum et modos diversos earum ex necessitate causae efficientis ponebant, sicut Avicenna, et eius sequaces. Posuit enim quod primum ens, in quantum intelligit se ipsum, producit unum tantum causatum, quod est intelligentia prima.\n\n**Translation**\n\nOthers, such as Avicenna and his followers, placed the plurality of things and their diverse modes of being under the **necessity of the efficient cause**. He held that the First Being, insofar as it understands itself, produces only **one immediate effect**, namely the first intelligence.\n\nThis is Aquinas's report of Avicenna's cosmological inference, not a verbatim quotation from Ibn Sīnā. The correct comparison pairs it with Ibn Sīnā's IX.4 and the explicit elaboration of separate intellects, not just with the general doctrine of causes.\n\n### Aquinas's first objection: God is not limited to one effect\n\n**Latin — *De potentia* III.16**\n\n> Sed haec etiam positio stare non potest. Primo, quia ponit potentiam divinam terminari ad unum effectum, qui est intelligentia prima.\n\n**Translation**\n\nThis position cannot stand: first, because it makes the divine power terminate in **only one effect**, the first intelligence.\n\n### The second objection: intermediary substances are not creators in their own right\n\n**Latin**\n\n> Secundo, quia ponit alias substantias praeter Deum esse aliarum creatrices, quod esse impossibile in alia quaestione, art. 4, ostensum est.\n\n**Translation**\n\nSecond, because it holds that substances other than God are **creators** of other substances; but we showed in article 4 that this is impossible.\n\nThis is an important distinction between **secondary causal agency** and **creation of existence absolutely**. Aquinas is not arguing that no angel or intelligence can affect another thing; he denies that a created intellect can be an independent source of creative power.\n\n### The third objection: rational order rather than restriction by natural necessity\n\n**Latin**\n\n> Sequitur etiam ad hanc positionem ... quod decor ordinis universi sit casualis, ex quo rerum diversitatem non adscribit intentioni finis, sed terminationi potentiarum activarum ad suos effectus.\n\n**Translation**\n\nIt also follows from this position, Aquinas says, that the beauty of the universe's order becomes **accidental**, since the diversity of things is not attributed to an intention ordered to an end, but merely to the restriction of productive powers to their effects.\n\n**A possible challenge to Aquinas:** Avicenna certainly recognises final causality, knowledge, cosmic hierarchy, and providence. Aquinas's criticism therefore cannot be taken as a historical demonstration that Avicenna simply **denied all finality**. Its precise force is whether the **diversity of this very cosmos** is intended as an end through unconstrained divine wisdom or instead follows necessarily from the degrees and limitations of emanated causes. The strongest formulation must engage Avicenna's account of *ʿināya* rather than an artificially stripped-down Avicennian cosmology.\n\n## V. Aquinas's positive account: divine wisdom, many effects, no natural necessity\n\n**Latin — *De potentia* III.16**\n\n> Quod enim Deus tale universum constituere voluerit, non est necessarium neque debitum, neque ex fine neque ex potentia efficientis, neque materiae ... Sed supposito quod tale universum producere voluerit, necessarium fuit quod tales et tales creaturas produxerit ...\n\n**Translation**\n\nThat God should will to establish **this particular cosmos** is neither necessary nor due to an end, to the power of the efficient cause, or to matter. **But given that God wills** to produce a cosmos of this kind, it becomes necessary for him to produce the particular creatures from which its intended constitution follows.\n\nHere Aquinas himself draws the essential modal distinction:\n\n- **Absolute necessity:** God had to produce this world.\n- **Conditional necessity:** if God has freely willed *this* world, then its required constituents and ordering follow.\n\nThe latter does not imply the former, and therefore the Thomistic account can affirm **necessary connections within a freely chosen causal order**.\n\n### Multiple things can proceed from one simple First\n\n**Latin**\n\n> Sic igitur dicendum est quod ab uno primo multitudo et diversitas creaturarum processit ... ex ordine sapientiae, ut in diversitate creaturarum perfectio consisteret universi.\n\n**Translation**\n\nTherefore we must say that from the one First Principle arose the plurality and diversity of creatures—not through material necessity or the limitation of productive power, but through the **ordering of wisdom**, so that the perfection of the cosmos should consist in the diversity of creatures.\n\n**Unification and division:** The First is absolutely simple; the many dependent effects do not constitute parts inside the First. The First can know diverse creatures and will their contingent existence without acquiring a new internal accident.\n\n---\n\n## VI. The precise AT–Avicennian differences\n\n| Issue | Avicennian text | Thomistic text | What can already be established |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Simplicity of the First | Absolutely simple and immutable | Absolutely simple and immutable | Real common commitment |\n| Knowledge of dependent order | First knows itself as principle; unknowing production rejected | Effects ordered by divine wisdom and will | Agreement that agency is not blind; differences over willing |\n| Necessary causal dependence | Complete enduring cause necessitates effect | Within freely willed cosmic order, necessary conditions follow | Important shared modal tools, distinct global inference |\n| Immediate plurality | Cosmology proceeds by mediation from a first separate intelligence | The First can immediately cause many without division | Real disagreement over necessary mediation |\n| Creative agency | Intelligence hierarchy has genuinely causal roles in cosmic production | Only God can create absolutely; intermediaries may act subordinately | Important difference needing qualified comparison |\n| Cosmic teleology | Order of being/providence through intellects | Diversity intended through wisdom and goodness | Both have teleology, differently grounded |\n| Free alternative worlds | IX.4's rejection of deliberation does not establish Thomistic alternative-willing | This cosmos was not absolutely necessary | Real disagreement or at least unresolved reconciliation |\n\n## VII. What would make a synthesis defensible?\n\nA mature comparative system must distinguish **four meanings of necessity**:\n\n1. Necessary in itself (*wājib bi-l-dhāt*).\n2. Necessary through an actually complete cause (*wājib bi-l-ghayr*).\n3. Necessary given the First's free willing of a particular order (*ex suppositione*).\n4. Necessary by an unfree production of one effect from divine nature.\n\nConfusing (2) with (4) makes Avicenna appear to deny freedom by a merely formal inference. Confusing (3) with (4) makes Aquinas appear equally necessitarian. The real disagreement concerns what additional **principle of cosmic specification**—knowledge and will, or intrinsic causal emanation—explains why the **particular** cosmic order obtains.\n\nA possible reconciliation can preserve Avicenna's layered causes and account of necessary *dependencies* while replacing the claim that the **First can only immediately produce one intelligence** with a Thomistic account of freely intended plurality. But this is a **new proposed synthesis**, not the doctrine already found in one historical thinker. The precise ramifications for the Giver of Forms, intellective cognition and divine science must then be reworked.\n\n## VIII. Remaining primary-text obligations\n\n- *Ilāhiyyāt* IX.4 beyond p.402 and IX.3 on the proceeding intellects; IX.6 on providence and evil.\n- Rāzī and Ṭūsī on divine willing, “from one only one proceeds,” and whether causal necessity implies determinism.\n- Taftāzānī, Jurjānī and Lāhījī's *Shawāriq* on free choice, divine knowledge and contingent events.\n- Narāqī's *Jāmiʿ al-afkār* on particular cognition, will and eternity, beyond the already-translated contingency passage.\n- Collation of physical manuscript witnesses and divergent prints for IX.4 variants (*maʿrifa*, *riḍā*).\n- Aquinas, *De potentia* III.15 (the divine will) and his broader *SCG* and *ST* discussions of creaturely agency.\n\n**Completion status:** a source-grounded argument comparison and selected continuous translations of major primary passages, with printed variants. It is **not a complete critical translation of the full historical schools or their doctrinal decisions**.\n"},{"id":"ch07","kind":"analysis","author":"GNAMMA scholarly editorial synthesis","work":"Late Avicennian Summa · comparative chapters","title":"Unity, plurality and divine knowledge","discipline":"Theology","locus":"Working chapter","lang":"mixed","family":"Comparative analysis","coverage":"Working analytical chapter with selected primary passages","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/gnamma-research/booksite/blob/master/research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/07-unity-plurality-and-divine-knowledge.md","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/07-unity-plurality-and-divine-knowledge.md","topics":["unity","existence","divine-will"],"sourceCoverage":"Selected Arabic/Latin excerpts; no full primary witness","manuscriptCollated":false,"md":"# Book IV, Chapter 2 — God, being, unity and plurality in Mahdī al-Narāqī\n**A critical translation of a decisive post-Sadrian argument from *Jāmiʿ al-afkār wa-nāqid al-anẓār* II:165–167**\n\n**Textual status:** A complete translation of the **bounded two-interpretation and response unit** from the latter part of II:166 to II:167, with its introductory quoted mystical argument from II:165–166 as selected excerpts. The Arabic base is printed below, separated by authorial voice. This is a translation and printed-text comparison, **not a fresh collation of manuscript witnesses**. The modern digital transcription includes artefacts marking page/copy breaks (e.g. DB, MA); they are removed from the normalized quotation without silently treating them as words of Narāqī.\n\n**Source:** [*Jāmiʿ al-afkār* vol. II, p.165](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9228/171?lang=ar), [p.166](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9228/172?lang=ar), [p.167](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9228/173?lang=ar).\n\n## 1. The preceding claim is *reported*—not yet Narāqī's conclusion\n\nNarāqī surveys the philosophical literature on the First's knowledge of existing things. He reports a statement from later interpreters that all the perfections of existents are included within the First's absolutely simple perfection and that the absolutely simple reality can therefore be called “all things.”\n\n**Arabic, II:165 — quoted/reported argument**\n\n> وبيانه ـ على ما ذكره بعض العرفاء المحققين ـ هو: أن كل ما هو بسيط الحقيقة فهو بوحدته كل الأشياء، ولا يخرج عنه شيء منها إلا ما هو من باب النقائص والأعدام والإمكانيات.\n\n**Translation**\n\nIts explanation—as stated by some accomplished mystical philosophers—is that whatever is absolutely simple in reality is, in its very unity, **all things**. Nothing is excluded from it except what belongs to deficiencies, privations and possibilities.\n\nThe argument contends that if the subject of a true negation were absolutely simple, it could not be excluded from any positive perfection without thereby possessing a privative determination or a division in itself. It concludes that the absolutely simple First encompasses all positive existential perfections and therefore knows them supremely.\n\n**Arabic — source, II:165**\n\n> فيثبت أن البسيط كل الموجودات من حيث الوجود والتمام لا من حيث النقائص والأعدام ولهذا ثبت علمه بالموجودات علماً بسيطاً وحضورها عنده على وجه أعلى وأتم.\n\n**Translation**\n\nIt follows that the absolutely simple reality is all existents **in respect of being and perfection**, not in respect of deficiency and privation. For this reason its knowledge of existents is a simple knowledge, and they are present to it in a higher and more perfect manner.\n\n**Voice warning:** The author here reports an argument **from other metaphysicians and mystics**. The next step is his analysis of whether their formulation entails numerical identity of God and creatures. A reader who quotes only the bold statement “the simple is all things” and labels it Narāqī's unqualified position will reverse the conclusion of the passage.\n\n## 2. The stronger statement of mystical unity — II:166\n\nNarāqī quotes or paraphrases the reported account of the First as pure existence, from which contingent things' existence and perfections emanate, and notes that privations and limitations belong to finite beings.\n\n**Arabic — reported position**\n\n> وتوضيح ذلك: أن واجب الوجود لما كان عين حقيقة الوجود بحيث لا يشوبه نقص ولا عدم ولا قصور ولا ماهية ولا تركيب بوجه من الوجوه، بل هو صرف الوجود ومحض الخيرية وعين الكمال، وكل وجود أو كمال وجود إنما هو يفيض عنه ويرشح منه ...\n\n**Translation**\n\nThe explanation is that the Necessary Existent is the very reality of existence, unmixed with deficiency, privation, limitation, quiddity or composition in any respect; rather, it is pure existence, pure goodness and perfection itself; and every existence and every existential perfection flows and emanates from it ...\n\n**Arabic — last words of reported account**\n\n> وذلك هو التوحيد الوجودي فاعرفه إن كنت أهلاً لذلك!؛ انتهى.\n\n**Translation**\n\n“That is existential unity [*al-tawḥīd al-wujūdī*]; understand it if you are qualified to do so.” **[End of the reported account.]**\n\nThe terminal «انتهى» explicitly closes the reported statement. The **subsequent** position is Narāqī's critical response.\n\n## 3. Narāqī's two interpretations — complete translated argument\n\n### Arabic base — II:166–167\n\n> وغير خفي أن ما ذكره بعض العرفاء يحتمل وجهين: أحدهما أن الواجب لما كان صرف الوجود وتأكده ووجودات الأشياء وجودات مشوبة بالقوة والعدم فائضة منه، وهذه الوجودات المشوبة بالعدم إذا فرض تحليلها إلى وجودات صرفة وعدماتها يكون وجود الواجب مشتملاً على تلك الوجودات الصرفة.\n>\n> يعني أن وجوده لما كان فوق التمام فيشتمل على كل مرتبة دونه مع الزيادة من غير أن يكون عين سائر المراتب، بمعنى أنه إذا حلل الأشياء الموجودة إلى وجوداتها الصرفة وأعدامها كان وجودها عين الواجب. وذلك كما أن السواد الأشد يشتمل على جميع السوادات التي تحته مع الزيادة من غير أن يكون الأشد عين سائر المراتب؛ فإذا قلنا أن السواد الأشد كل السوادات، يكون المراد أن كل مرتبة دون الأشد يوجد في الأشد مع الزيادة، لا أن يكون الأشد عين باقي المراتب.\n>\n> وثانيهما أن يكون المراد أن وجود الواجب هو عين وجودات الممكنات إذا خلصت عن أعدامها. وأنت خبير بأن مبنى الوجه الأول على كون الوجود مقولاً بالتشكيك، وكون وجود الواجب فرداً أقوى وأشد منه؛ ومبنى الثاني على وحدة الوجود بل الموجود؛ والقول بكل منهما يشتمل على مفاسد، وقد بينا ذلك بما لا مزيد عليه في رسالتنا المسماة «قرة العيون».\n>\n> فالحق أن مراد المعلمين من أن الواجب كل الوجود مع وحدته البسيطة الصرفة: أن جميع الوجودات لوازم ذاته ورشحات وجوده وهو علة تامة لها، وهي ناشئة صادرة عنها، وهو كل الأشياء في الشهود العلمي بمعنى أن حضور ذاته عند ذاته هو حضور الأشياء، بل هو في انكشاف الأشياء أقوى من حضورها بأنفسها.\n>\n> فمن قال أن الأشياء عين الواجب أراد بالعينية العينية باعتبار الشهود العلمي وباعتبار ملزوميته وعليته لها وانطوائها فيه إجمالاً، وهذا المعنى هو مرادهم من قولهم إنه مجمل الأشياء أو كلها، لا أن تكون تلك الوجودات عين وجود الواجب بعد تخليصها عن أعدامها الإمكانية أو يكون وجود الواجب مشتملاً عليها مع الزيادة؛ لأنه إن تحقق حينئذ بين وجود الواجب وسائر الوجودات ما به الاشتراك وما به الامتياز لزم التركيب؛ وإن لم يكن بينهما ما به الامتياز أصلاً لزم وحدة الوجود؛ وإن لم يكن بينهما ما به الاشتراك بل كانا متخالفين بأنفسهما لم يكن لاشتمال وجود الواجب على سائر الوجودات معنى.\n\n### English — connected translation\n\nIt is clear that the statement reported from certain mystical philosophers admits **two interpretations**.\n\nThe first is that, because the Necessary Existent is **pure and intensified existence**, whereas the existences of things are mixed with potentiality and privation and flow from it, if those existences were analytically separated into **pure existence** and their privations, the existence of the Necessary Existent would encompass their pure existences.\n\nThat is to say: because its existence transcends all ordinary completeness, it encompasses every lower degree **with a surplus of perfection**, without being numerically identical to those lower degrees. On this reading, were actual things analytically divided into their pure existences and their privations, their existential perfection would be identical [in the relevant respect] with that of the Necessary Existent. This resembles the way the most intense black contains all lesser degrees of blackness with an additional intensity, without being numerically identical to each weaker shade. Thus, if one says that the most intense black is “all blackness,” the meaning is that every lower degree is included within the more intense shade **plus something further**, not that the most intense shade is literally each of the others.\n\nThe second interpretation is that the existence of the Necessary Existent is **numerically identical with the existences of contingent things**, once their privations are removed.\n\nYou should recognise that the first interpretation rests upon the **gradation of existence** (*tashkīk al-wujūd*) and upon the Necessary Existent being its most intense and strongest instance. The second rests upon the **unity of existence**, indeed the unity of the **existent**. Each of these two positions, Narāqī says, entails serious difficulties, which he has explained at length in his treatise entitled ***Qurrat al-ʿUyūn***.\n\nThe correct interpretation of the teachers' claim that the Necessary Existent is “all existence” while remaining absolutely simple and one is this: **all existences are necessary concomitants of its essence and emanations of its existence**. It is their complete cause, and they proceed from it. It is “all things” in the order of **intellectual witnessing or divine knowledge**, meaning that its own self-presence to itself is also the presence of things; indeed, its disclosure of things is more complete than their presence to themselves.\n\nThus, anyone who says that “things are identical with the Necessary Existent” must mean **identity with respect to the intelligible witnessing in which they are known**, and with respect to the First's being their necessary ground and complete cause, in whose perfection they are contained **in an undifferentiated way**. That is what is meant by saying that the First **comprehends** all things or **is all things**.\n\nIt does **not** mean that those finite existences become numerically identical to the Necessary Existent's existence when stripped of their contingent privations, nor that the existence of the Necessary Existent contains them simply as degrees plus a greater degree.\n\nFor if one posited between the Necessary Existent and the other existences **something shared** alongside **something that differentiates them**, this would entail **composition** in the First. If there were **nothing whatsoever** differentiating them, one would collapse into numerical **unity of existence**. But if there were no common respect at all and the two were utterly unrelated in themselves, then the assertion that the Necessary Existent **encompasses** other existences would lose its meaning.\n\n**Editorial note:** The English qualification “[in the relevant respect]” in the first interpretation flags the tension in Narāqī's original compressed exposition: he reports “identity” in the reconstructed argument yet uses a graded-black analogy to deny numerical identity of levels. It is not a doctrinal correction silently put into his mouth.\n\n## 4. Three competing ontologies, with their real costs\n\n| Interpretation | Strength | Narāqī's objection | Further philosophical task |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Graded existential participation**: God is strongest instance of one existential reality | Explains participation, the recurrence of perfections and analogical unity | Shared principle plus differentiating degree may introduce complexity into the absolutely simple First | Can analogy avoid treating divine existence as a species within a genus or scale? |\n| **Numerical monism**: purified finite existence is God's own existence | Radical explanation of dependent being and divine immanence | Distinction between the Necessary and contingent existents collapses | Can real plurality and independent substantial natures survive? |\n| **Causal and epistemic containment**: all finite existences proceed from and are known through the First | Preserves derivative reality and First's uncompounded perfection | Must show how the First is present to all without a multiplicity of intrinsic representations | Requires theory of divine knowledge, causality and analogy |\n\nThe remarkable point is Narāqī's refusal simply to accept the first choice because it seems less monistic. He worries that **gradation itself** may make God's existence an instance within a larger shared ontological class, risking composition; this is a different pressure from a straightforward objection to mystical monism.\n\n## 5. Thomistic parallels and differences\n\nCompare Aquinas, [*De potentia* III.16, ad 3–4 and ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/qdp3.html), on the one and the many, the distinct kinds of unity, and the ability of the one First to cause differentiated beings.\n\n### A. Causal containment is not numerical identity\n\nAquinas says the First causes all beings and their diverse modes because **the source of being as being** cannot leave the determinations of being wholly unexplained. This resembles Narāqī's refusal to dissolve derivative existents into the existence of their source.\n\n### B. Participation is neither a genus nor simple univocity\n\nThe standard AT approach is that finite beings **participate in being** and God is not a generic maximal case within the same genus. It therefore attempts to avoid the argument that graded existence forces God into a common externally differentiated species.\n\nThis is promising but not identical to Narāqī's account. His positive reading stresses **scientific/intellectual witnessing** (*shuhūd ʿilmī*) and the necessary emanation of all creatures as concomitants of God's self-presence. Aquinas stresses divine knowledge and willing without the claim that the First is cosmologically necessitated to produce this actual order.\n\n### C. Divine knowledge without real multiplicity\n\nNarāqī interprets “all things are present in the First” as the First's **knowing itself**, whose perfection contains the ground of all things. Aquinas likewise holds that God's knowledge of things is not a collection of accidental representations subsequently acquired. But the mode of creaturely dependence and the necessity or freedom of cosmic production remain contested.\n\n### D. Why “one existence” is an ambiguous phrase\n\nA mature summa should state exactly which of these is being asserted:\n\n- **one intelligible meaning of existence** (conceptual unity);\n- **graded participation in being** (analogical/metaphysical structure);\n- **one source of all existence** (causal unity);\n- **one existential act numerically identical in all things** (strong monism);\n- **all existents known at once in God's simple self-knowledge** (epistemic unity).\n\nOnly the fourth is directly a claim of **numerical identity** of all contingent existence with divine existence. Narāqī endorses the third and fifth here while rejecting the simplistic formulations of the second and fourth.\n\n## 6. A historical correction to the previous GNAMMA research\n\nThe earlier statement “Narāqī rejects *waḥdat al-wujūd*” is **too blunt**. He reports and interprets an **Akbarian/perfectionist** formula, distinguishes two metaphysical readings and rejects the corresponding strong claims, but then retains a powerful **causal and epistemic unity**. This is not merely an anti-mystical position.\n\nLikewise, the fact that a contemporary modern historian calls his position “transformed Avicennism” does not imply wholesale fidelity to the earlier Ibn Sīnā. At the level of divine knowledge, his *Jāmiʿ* exhibits a sophisticated post-Sadrian, Akbarian-aware philosophical problem even when rejecting some forms of ontological unity.\n\n## 7. What remains\n\n1. **Primary source cross-check:** identify the exact “teachers” and earlier mystical authorities he paraphrases; do not attribute the argument to Ibn ʿArabī, Qūnawī or Ṣadrā by guess.\n2. **Independent treatise:** locate and collate *Qurrat al-ʿUyūn*, which Narāqī explicitly identifies as having supplied the stronger objections. The title itself does not establish edition or manuscript witness.\n3. **Divine knowledge:** translate the succeeding arguments about Fārābī, Bahmanyār and Avicenna on global knowledge and detailed forms, not just the reported formula.\n4. **Aquinas:** collate *De veritate* q.2, *ST* I qq.14–15, *De potentia* III.16 and the *SCG* divine knowledge chapters.\n5. **Ṣadrā:** distinguish gradation of existence from the “personal unity of being” in his mature synthesis and the role of intelligible emanation.\n6. **Variant readings:** this is based on one published running text; typography and modern page-break artefacts have been normalised. No manuscript variants are claimed.\n\n**Completion status:** one bounded multi-page, complete argumentative unit in Narāqī's own interpretive response (II:166–167) translated with its reported positions marked; comparative implications to AT constructed; broader chapter on God's knowledge and the cited *Qurrat al-ʿUyūn* not yet independently collated.\n"},{"id":"ch08","kind":"analysis","author":"GNAMMA scholarly editorial synthesis","work":"Late Avicennian Summa · comparative chapters","title":"Universals, quiddity and nafs al-amr","discipline":"Logic","locus":"Working chapter","lang":"mixed","family":"Comparative analysis","coverage":"Working analytical chapter with selected primary passages","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/gnamma-research/booksite/blob/master/research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/08-universals-quiddity-nafs-al-amr.md","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/08-universals-quiddity-nafs-al-amr.md","topics":["universals","mental-existence","quiddity"],"sourceCoverage":"Selected Arabic/Latin excerpts; no full primary witness","manuscriptCollated":false,"md":"# Book I, Chapter 3 — Universals, the three considerations of quiddity, and *nafs al-amr*\n**Ibn Sīnā, *Ilāhiyyāt al-Shifāʾ* V.1, pp.195–196, read together with Lāhījī's mental-existence analysis and Thomistic universals**\n\n**Textual status:** Continuous, directly source-linked translation of two *bounded argument units*: (a) the three senses of universal at *Ilāhiyyāt* V.1 p.195, and (b) the passage on horseness *qua horseness* at p.196, along with published printed variants. **Not** a full translation of the whole of V.1–2, and **not** an assertion that *nafs al-amr* is a term employed in these exact passages. It is introduced below as a later problem *requiring* further evidence.\n\n**Primary texts:** [Ibn Sīnā, *Ilāhiyyāt* V.1 p.195](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/195), [p.196](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/196); [Lāhījī, *Shawāriq* I:50–52](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9309/52?lang=ar); [Aquinas, *ST* I q.85 a.2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html) and Aristotle's *De interpretatione* and *De anima* commentaries (comparative loci, not full Latin edition here).\n\n## I. The universality of a concept has three distinguishable meanings\n\n### Arabic — Ibn Sīnā, V.1 p.195 (the whole threefold classification)\n\n> وبالحري أن نتكلم الآن في الكلي والجزئي، فإنه مناسب أيضاً لما فرغنا منه، وهو من الأعراض الخاصة بالوجود، فنقول: إن الكلي قد يقال على وجوه ثلاثة:\n>\n> فيقال كلي للمعنى من جهة أنه مقول بالفعل على كثيرين، مثل الإنسان.\n>\n> ويقال كلي للمعنى إذا كان جائزاً أن يحمل على كثيرين وإن لم يشترط أنهم موجودون بالفعل، مثل معنى البيت المسبع، فإنه كلي من حيث إن من طبيعته أن يقال على كثيرين، ولكن ليس يجب أن يكون أولئك الكثيرين لا محالة موجودين بل ولا الواحد منهم.\n>\n> ويقال كلي للمعنى الذي لا مانع من تصوره أن يقال على كثيرين، إنما يمنع منه إن منع سبب ويدل عليه دليل، مثل الشمس والأرض، فإنها من حيث تعقل شمساً وأرضاً لا يمنع الذهن عن أن يجوز أن معناها يوجد في كثير، إلا أن يأتيه دليل أو حجة يعرف به أن هذا ممتنع. ويكون ذلك ممتنعاً بسبب من خارج لا لنفس تصوره.\n\n### English — connected translation\n\nIt is appropriate now to discuss **the universal and the particular**, since this is related to what we have just treated, and universality and particularity belong among the features associated with existence.\n\nWe say that the term **universal** can be used in three ways.\n\nFirst, a meaning is called universal insofar as it **is actually predicated of many things**—as, for example, the meaning *human being*.\n\nSecond, a meaning is called universal if it **can be predicated of many things**, without requiring that those many things actually exist—as with the meaning of a **seven-sided house**. It is universal because its nature permits predication of many things, though there is no necessity that the many actually exist, or even that a single instance exist.\n\nThird, a meaning is called universal if **nothing in its conception prevents its being predicated of many things**, even if some further reason or evidence prevents it—as in the meanings *sun* and *earth*. So far as these are understood as sun and earth, the mind is not prohibited from thinking that their meaning could be instantiated in many things; only a further argument or demonstration may establish that this is impossible. Such impossibility would arise from a reason **external to the bare conception**, not from the concept's nature as conceived.\n\n### Distinguish these propositions\n\n- **Actual predicability** over many existing individuals is different from **possible predicability** with no current individuals.\n- **Conceptual non-contradiction of multiple instances** is different from a *demonstrated impossibility* arising from an extrinsic metaphysical or cosmological thesis.\n- Therefore being a **universal intelligible** is not a property that requires many extramental instantiations or a real separated universal existing in a third realm.\n\nA supposed “universal” that happens to have a single instance is not necessarily internally contradictory.\n\n## II. What a thing is *in itself* is not intrinsically either a mental universal or an existing individual\n\n### Arabic — V.1 p.196, through the passage on horseness\n\n> وقد يمكن أن يجمع هذا كله في أن هذا الكلي هو الذي لا يمنع نفس تصوره عن أن يقال على كثيرين. ويجب أن يكون الكلي المستعمل في المنطق وما أشبهه هو هذا.\n>\n> وأما الجزئي المفرد فهو الذي نفس تصوره يمنع أن يقال معناه على كثيرين، كذات زيد هذا المشار إليه، فإنه مستحيل أن تتوهم إلا له وحده.\n>\n> فالكلي من حيث هو كلي شيء، و«من حيث هو شيء تلحقه الكلية» شيء. فالكلي من حيث هو كلي هو ما يدل عليه أحد هذه الحدود، فإذا كان ذلك إنساناً أو فرساً فهناك معنى آخر غير معنى الكلية وهو الفرسية. فإن حد الفرسية ليس حد الكلية، ولا الكلية داخلة في حد الفرسية، فإن الفرسية لها حد لا يفتقر إلى حد الكلية لكن تعرض له الكلية.\n>\n> فإنه في نفسه ليس شيء من الأشياء البتة إلا الفرسية، فإنه في نفسه لا واحد ولا كثير ولا موجود في الأعيان ولا في النفس ولا في شيء من ذلك بالقوة ولا بالفعل على أن يكون داخلاً في الفرسية، بل من حيث هو فرسية فقط. بل الواحدية صفة تقترن إلى الفرسية، فتكون الفرسية مع تلك الصفة واحدة.\n>\n> كذلك للفرسية مع تلك الصفة صفات أخرى كثيرة داخلة عليها، فالفرسية بشرط أنها تطابق بحدها أشياء كثيرة تكون عامة، ولأنها مأخوذة بخواص وأعراض مشار إليها تكون خاصة. فالفرسية في نفسها فرسية فقط.\n\n### English — connected translation\n\nAll these senses can be brought together under the statement that **a universal is something whose very conception does not prevent its being predicated of many things**. This is the sense of “universal” required in logic and related disciplines.\n\nA **singular particular**, by contrast, is something whose very conception prevents the relevant meaning from applying to many—for example the individual Zayd, this person to whom one points, whose particularity cannot be imagined as belonging to anyone other than himself.\n\nNow a universal **considered as universal** is one thing, and the thing **to which universality belongs** is another. Universality as such is what the preceding definitions indicate. If that to which universality belongs is a human or a horse, there is another meaning besides the meaning of universality itself: namely, **horseness**. The definition of horseness is not the definition of universality; nor is universality included in the definition of horseness. Horseness possesses its own definition, which does not depend upon the definition of universality, although **universality may belong to it**.\n\nFor horseness in itself is **nothing at all other than horseness**. Considered in itself, it is neither one nor many, nor existent among external individuals nor existent in the soul, nor any of these things potentially or actually **as a constituent within the meaning of horseness**. It is simply horseness insofar as it is horseness.\n\nUnity is, rather, a determination that can accompany horseness, so that horseness together with this determination is one. Likewise, many other determinations can accompany horseness together with unity. When horseness is taken under the condition of its definition corresponding to many things, it is general; but when taken together with individualising and demonstrable features, it is particular. **Horseness in itself is simply horseness.**\n\n### A crucial clarification\n\nThe sentence “horseness in itself is neither externally nor mentally existent” does **not** mean that an independently established **nonexistent horseness** inhabits a third ontological region. It means that the definition of horseness as such is not the definition of **mental existence**, **external existence**, **universality**, or **individuality**.\n\nOne may then ask how this nature is actually present **in an intellectual act**, and how it is individuated **within actual horses**. The formal consideration abstracts from those modes without creating a new concrete entity.\n\nThis matters for the broader debates about Muʿtazilī *shayʾiyyat al-maʿdūm*, conceptual being, *nafs al-amr*, and Ṣadrā's mental existence: none can simply be deduced from a compressed slogan “essence exists before existence.”\n\n## III. Printed witnesses and textual variants\n\nThe online edition provides a variant apparatus for the printed base:\n\n| Printed p. | Lemma | Reported variants | Implication |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 195 | «فإنها» referring to sun and earth | «فإنهما» ج، د، ص، ط | Grammatical agreement with the dual examples; helps parse referent |\n| 195 | «كثيرين» | «كثير» in several witnesses | Pure grammatical variant |\n| 195 | «أن» in «من حيث أن» | omitted by ب، ج، د، ص، م | Does not alter the threefold classification |\n| 196 | «فالكلي من حيث هو كلي» | «كل» in ط، م | Potentially relevant to first metaphysical distinction |\n| 196 | «ولا موجود» | «ولا موجود لا» in د | Additional negation may introduce grammatical awkwardness |\n| 196 | «من حيث هو فرسية» | «هي من حيث هي فرسية» ج، د; «هي من حيث فرسية» ص، ط، م | Different pronouns preserving the point that essence is essence alone |\n| 196 | «تقترن» | «تقترب» ط | Change from conjunction to approach, potentially a printing or copying slip |\n\nThese are **variants reported by the edition**, not manuscript readings independently consulted by this translator. Do not claim a new *taḥqīq* based merely on transcribing editorial sigla.\n\n## IV. How later Lāhījī develops the problem\n\nIn [Chapter 1](01-mental-existence-and-quiddity.md), Lāhījī distinguishes three questions:\n\n1. **What the concept is about**—a quiddity such as horseness, substance, unity or colour.\n2. **How it exists mentally**—a form in intellect, with a particular mode of intentional presence.\n3. **The act of knowing as an actual quality** of the existing intellect.\n\nHis *Shawāriq* I:50–52 explicitly resists the inference that the represented essence is literally an extramental substance present in someone's skull or that the real psychological act's categorical quality determines the nature represented.\n\nThe development is not merely chronological repetition. Lāhījī's formulation connects **Avicenna's indifference of quiddity in itself** to the logical distinction between **what is understood** and **the mode in which the understanding inheres**.\n\n## V. Comparing Aquinas without confusing terms\n\nAquinas's doctrine of intellective species as *id quo intelligitur* and the nature as primarily *quod intelligitur* is strongly relevant. Both frameworks reject identifying the **extramental object of knowledge** with the **mental act's categorical status**.\n\nBut neither provides an instant proof that Avicennian mental existence is numerically identical to Thomistic intentional presence. Their differences about the Active Intellect, the ontological category of mental representations, and how the nature attains universal intentio must be separately explored.\n\n**Further Thomistic question:** An essence considered absolutely need not be actually instantiated. But how does the intellect's concept of a horse relate to **the nature's actuality in the horse**, and to the **divine idea** of the horse? Do not confuse those three levels with an independent third **realm of nonexistent quiddities**.\n\n## VI. Toward a rigorous theory of *nafs al-amr*\n\nThe **objective factual status** of true propositions (*nafs al-amr*) is broader than actual things presently existing in the extramental world. Mathematics, universal conditionals, modal claims, and truths about absence or non-existence make this obvious.\n\nBut one cannot solve the problem simply by postulating an extra universe where every true proposition is eternally stored. A suitable theory should distinguish:\n\n- **What makes a statement true**: a relation to being, essence, formal determination, mathematical structure, or the absence of what is denied;\n- **Where the intellectual act is**: a knower's mind;\n- **What it signifies or represents**: a possible, actual or impossible object;\n- **Whether its truth requires an actual worldly referent**: which varies by statement form;\n- **What it means for the truth to hold independently of anyone's judgement**.\n\nThe present Ibn Sīnā passage supports the **distinctions** needed to frame this enquiry. It does **not** state a late scholastic doctrine of *nafs al-amr* verbatim. That doctrine needs the *Tajrīd* and later logic commentaries (including those already studied in the separate GNAMMA and Asad Q. Ahmed projects).\n\n## VII. A connected contribution to the AT project\n\nLogical universal → quiddity conceptually considered → actual existence under mental/extramental modes → individuation → cognition of the nature → truth of predication → *nafs al-amr*.\n\nThe order prevents these common inferences:\n\n- from **conceivability** to a **third realm of real uninstantiated essences**;\n- from **mental existence** to the **existence of the understood object in the knower as a physical substance**;\n- from **one meaning shared across many** to **one numerically identical real object in them**;\n- from the **unity of an existential concept** to the **numerical identity of all created beings with God**.\n\nThe next question is whether existence as a **metaphysical actuality** is really distinct from each quiddity, and what the cause does in conferring actuality. [The subsequent making debate](02-making-and-causal-explanation.md) addresses that problem.\n\n**Completion status:** full translation of the selected V.1 opening logical classification and the p.196 horseness argument; printed text-variant apparatus; comparative interpretation. The rest of V.1, V.2, and later *nafs al-amr* witnesses remain to be critically translated.\n"},{"id":"ch09","kind":"analysis","author":"GNAMMA scholarly editorial synthesis","work":"Late Avicennian Summa · comparative chapters","title":"Mawāqif / Maqāṣid on matter and cognition","discipline":"Kalām","locus":"Working chapter","lang":"mixed","family":"Comparative analysis","coverage":"Working analytical chapter with selected primary passages","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/gnamma-research/booksite/blob/master/research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/09-mawaqif-maqasid-hylomorphism-mental-existence.md","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/09-mawaqif-maqasid-hylomorphism-mental-existence.md","topics":["matter","mental-existence","natural-generation"],"sourceCoverage":"Selected Arabic/Latin excerpts; no full primary witness","manuscriptCollated":false,"md":"# Book II, Chapter 3 — Did the later kalām commentators preserve Avicennian matter and mental existence?\n**Direct text comparison: al-Ījī's *Mawāqif*, al-Jurjānī's *Sharḥ*, al-Taftāzānī's *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*, Lāhījī and the AT system**\n\n**Edition and voice status:** Direct selected Arabic–English argument units from (1) the nested *Mawāqif / Sharḥ al-Mawāqif / ḥawāshī* edition, vol. VII pp.34–35, 45, and (2) al-Taftāzānī's *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid* pp.78–79. The latter is a searchable **1981** edition, distinct from the composition date. The *Mawāqif* display interleaves **matn in parentheses, running commentary, and two later glosses**; page-level divisions alone do not determine the authorship of every visible explanatory phrase. I therefore label translations “layered commentary witness” unless the source precisely identifies the lemma. **Not** a full translation or manuscript collation of either work.\n\n**Primary locators:**\n- [al-Ījī/Jurjānī with Siyālkūtī/Ḥalabī, vol. VII p.34](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9300/34?lang=ar)\n- [p.35](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9300/35?lang=ar)\n- [p.45](https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9300/45?lang=ar)\n- [al-Taftāzānī, *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*, printed p.78](https://usul.ai/ar/t/sharh-maqasid/78)\n- [p.79](https://usul.ai/ar/t/sharh-maqasid/79)\n\n## I. The original hylomorphic demonstration from division and continuity\n\n### Matn, gloss, and explanation must be kept distinct\n\nThe passage in *Mawāqif* VII:34 argues that corporeal extension is not by itself the whole of the body's reality. When one connected body is divided, the continuous unity is destroyed, yet something remains receptive of separation and connection.\n\n**Arabic lemma, p.34 — embedded matn**\n\n> فان ثمة أمرا قابلا للاتصال تارة والانفصال أخرى ... وهذا الأمر الذي هو قابل للانفصال ومغاير للاتصال هو الذي نسميه بالهيولى.\n\n**English translation**\n\nThere must be something capable at one time of **connection** and at another of **separation**. That which receives separation and is distinct from the connected condition is what we call **prime matter** (*hayūlā*).\n\n**Arabic lemma, p.34 — embedded explanation**\n\n> فالقابل للاتصال والانفصال يغاير كلا منهما.\n\n**English translation**\n\nThe underlying subject receptive of connection and separation is distinct from either of those determinations.\n\nThe argument has three steps:\n\n1. A connected body can become separated.\n2. The **same receptivity** must be distinguished from the opposed determinations of connection and separation, since what is destroyed cannot simply be the very subject that remains receptive.\n3. Therefore, beyond the corporeal form of extension considered as an actuality, a **material principle** is posited.\n\nThis is an intelligible proof for hylomorphism, not a verbal stipulation that matter exists. But the argument requires a substantive premise about continuity and persistence.\n\n### The first objection: why not corporeal form itself?\n\nThe later discussion (p.45) points out that the corporeal form of a body, while it entails some extension, need not entail **one determinate magnitude**. Perhaps the same corporeal actuality can receive varied magnitudes. If so, the argument has not demonstrated a **second** entity called prime matter.\n\n**Arabic — p.45**\n\n> وجوابه أن الصورة الجسمية وإن كانت مستلزمة في الوجود والتعقل للمقدار إلا أنها لا تستلزم مقداراً مخصوصاً، فجاز أن تكون هي قابلة لتلك المقادير المختلفة فلا يثبت وجود أمر آخر.\n\n**English translation**\n\nThe reply is that, although corporeal form involves magnitude both in being and in intelligibility, it does not entail any **specific** magnitude. It could therefore itself receive the differing magnitudes, and there would be no proof that some further principle exists.\n\n**Critical distinction:** The exchange exposes a problem common to Avicennism and Thomism: **what exactly persists** when a body changes its dimensions, continuity, or substantial form? Establishing that some receptive subject persists does not by itself establish that the subject is **pure potency** rather than corporeal substance with some actuality.\n\n## II. The objection from substantial generation is not an automatic victory\n\nA natural objection is that, in generation and corruption, one substantial form is taken off and another put on; a persisting matter must underlie those forms.\n\n**Arabic — p.45, layered text**\n\n> والكون والفساد ... إذ لا بد فيهما من أمر يخلع صورة ويلبس أخرى وهو الهيولى.\n\n**English translation**\n\nGeneration and corruption seem to require something that removes one form and receives another: that would be prime matter.\n\nThe counterargument questions this inference by suggesting that what changes are the **specific substantial forms**, and perhaps corporeal form could itself be the receptive subject. It adds that if the phenomenon of generation is already explained by assuming prime matter, using it to **prove** prime matter would be circular.\n\n**Arabic — p.45**\n\n> على أنا نقول وجود هذه الأمور التي استعين بها مبنى على وجود الهيولى فيلزم الدور.\n\n**English translation**\n\nMoreover, we say that the existence of the very things appealed to in the proof rests on [the assumption of] prime matter; circular reasoning would follow.\n\n### A genuine late-kalām challenge\n\nThe kalām commentators are not merely saying “Aristotle asserts matter, therefore matter exists.” They are exposing a possible **begging of the question** in one traditional hylomorphic demonstration. A Thomist must answer with a non-circular argument from potency, substantial continuity, or change, rather than invoke inherited terminology.\n\n## III. The theologians' trilemma against matter — p.45\n\nThe display introduces what it identifies as a principal argument among theologians against prime matter. Matter either has **location** or it does not. If it has location independently, it appears already to be a body. If located through dependence on corporeal form, it looks like an attribute of form rather than the substrate receiving form. If it lacks location altogether, the relation of bodily form to it becomes obscure.\n\n**Arabic — three core clauses**\n\n> أما أن يكون لها حصول في الحيز أو لا ... فإن كان ... على سبيل الاستقلال فجسم ... [وإن] على سبيل التبعية للصورة الجسمية فالهيولى حينئذ صفة حالة في الجسمية ... وإن لم يكن لها حصول في الحيز ... فلا تختص الجسمية بها.\n\n**English translation**\n\nEither [prime matter] exists in a spatial region or it does not. If it has spatial emplacement independently, it would already be a **body**. If it is spatially located only in dependence upon corporeal form, matter would instead appear to be an **attribute inhering in corporeal form**. If it has no spatial emplacement at all, it seems impossible for corporeal form to pertain specifically to it.\n\n**Voice warning:** The page includes subordinate objections and corrective glosses (including questioning the inference from similarity of the two elements to their strict identity). The trilemma is therefore a **reported theologians' argument within a layered edition**, not demonstrated as al-Jurjānī's final position simply by its presence in his commentary.\n\n### Reply to be tested\n\nThe strongest Peripatetic reply would say that prime matter is **not itself an actually spatial body**, but is the **potential material principle of a body**, and that its emplacement belongs to it **through the actual composite** without making it an accident of corporeal form. The trilemma could therefore conflate “is in a place as a constituent of an actual body” with “has its own independently actual spatial extension.”\n\nThat reply is **editorial reconstruction**; it is **not** being attributed to a particular commentator until the response in a controlled primary edition is translated.\n\n## IV. *Maqāṣid*: why the concepts of nonexistent things require careful epistemology\n\nThe *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid* pp.78–79 treats **mental existence** (*wujūd dhihnī*) in direct relation to universal affirmative judgements and things without extramental instances.\n\n### The argument from universals and affirmative judgements — p.78\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> الثاني أن الكلي مفهوم وكل مفهوم ثابت ضرورة تميزه عند العقل ... وليس في الخارج لأن كل ما هو في الخارج مشخص فيكون في الذهن.\n\n**English translation**\n\nSecond, the universal is understood, and everything understood has some established status, because it is distinguished by intellect. Yet it cannot be established as a universal outside the mind, since everything actual outside the mind is individuated. It must therefore be established **mentally**.\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> الثالث أن من القضايا موجبة حقيقية وهي تستدعي وجود الموضوع ضرورة ... كقولنا كل عنقاء حيوان.\n\n**English translation**\n\nThird, some propositions are genuinely affirmative, and they seem to require the existence of a subject; yet that subject may have no actual extramental instance, as in “Every phoenix is an animal.”\n\nA further point follows: sciences use universal judgements extending over all possible members, not just the presently actual specimens.\n\n### The opposing reply: truth need not mean an independent internal image\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> وأعترض بأنا لا نسلم أن الإيجاب يقتضي وجود الموضوع ... بل اللازم هو تميز الموضوع والمحمول عند العقل بمعنى تصورهما.\n\n**English translation**\n\nAn objection is raised: we do not concede that affirmative predication requires the actual being of the subject. Rather, it requires only that the subject and predicate be distinguished in the intellect, through their conception.\n\nThe objection then charges the advocate of mental existence with **assuming what is disputed**: if one argues that understanding a thing proves it is present as a form in the mind, one is already assuming the very theory under dispute.\n\n**Arabic — p.78**\n\n> وهذا نفس المتنازع لأن القول بكون التعقل بالحصول في العقل إنما هو رأي القائلين بالوجود الذهني.\n\n**English translation**\n\nThat is precisely what is in dispute: saying that intellection consists in something's obtaining within the intellect is already the view of those who affirm mental existence.\n\n### The response and its limit\n\nThe main response insists that intellectual apprehension requires a relation between knower and known and that pure nonbeing cannot serve as a term of such a relation. Since the known may lack extramental being, its suitable mode of establishment must be **mental**.\n\nBut p.79 then raises competing hypotheses: perhaps forms exist in **Platonic separated Ideas**, or are present in a separate **Active Intellect**, instead of being internally actual in the human subject. These possibilities must be addressed rather than silently excluded.\n\n## V. Three objections against mental existence — *Maqāṣid* p.79\n\nThe later part of p.79 presents **three very precise objections**.\n\n### 1. If heat is in intellect, does the intellect become hot?\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> لو كان تصور الشيء مستلزماً لحصوله في العقل لزم من تصور الحرارة والبرودة أن يكون الذهن حاراً وبارداً.\n\n**English translation**\n\nIf conceiving a thing meant its obtaining in the intellect, conceiving heat and cold would make the intellect **hot and cold**.\n\n### 2. If heaven is understood, must the whole immense cosmos be physically inside the mind?\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> أنه يلزم أن تحصل السماوات بعظمها في العقل عند تعقلها الكل وفي الخيال عند تخيلها.\n\n**English translation**\n\nIt would follow that the vast heavens themselves obtain inside the intellect when the intellect knows them, or inside the imagination when they are imagined.\n\n### 3. Does a nonexistent thing become extramentally real simply because the mind is physically real?\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> أنه يلزم من تعقل المعدومات وجودها في الخارج لكونها موجودة في العقل الموجود في الخارج.\n\n**English translation**\n\nIt would follow that nonexistent things become extramentally existent when we understand them, because they would be inside an intellect that itself exists outside the mind.\n\n### The primary reply: differentiate a thing's real *identity* from its intellectual *form*\n\n**Arabic — p.79**\n\n> والجواب أن مبنى الكل على عدم التفرقة بين الوجود المتأصل الذي به الهوية العينية وغير المتأصل الذي به الصورة العقلية.\n\n**English translation**\n\nThe answer is that all three objections fail to distinguish **rooted or proper existence**, through which a thing has its real extramental identity, from the non-primary mode of existence proper to its **intelligible form**.\n\n**Arabic**\n\n> وبالجملة فماهية الشيء أعني صورته العقلية مخالفة لهويته العينية في كثير من اللوازم.\n\n**English translation**\n\nIn short, the quiddity of a thing understood as its intellectual form differs from its **real external individual identity** in many of their concomitants.\n\nThis is not the thesis that knowing a horse forces a physical horse into one's brain. It is a differentiation of **what is signified**, **the intellectual form**, and **the body's external actuality**.\n\n### A genuine historical route to Lāhījī\n\nLāhījī *Shawāriq* I:50–52 does not suddenly invent the distinction between an intellectual form and external reality. The *Maqāṣid* had already stated the core objection and reply in a scholastic form. Lāhījī develops additional distinctions concerning the **particular psychic actuality** of the knowing act, the represented object's categorical nature, and its mode of intentional existence.\n\nThe question for our historical narrative is thus not “who first invented mental existence?” but **how the commentators refined the semantic and ontological relation between a mental representation, its content, and the actual known thing**.\n\n## VI. Connections to the four causes and the AT comparison\n\n**Natural philosophy:** The *Mawāqif* passage tests Avicennian matter/form theory at the level of its most fundamental **proof**, not just its terminology. An AT philosopher defending prime matter must respond to the trilemma without treating matter as a tiny hidden spatial body.\n\n**Epistemology:** The *Maqāṣid* passage tests the **intelligible representation** without turning it into an extramental body or a mere arbitrary mental name. Aquinas's distinction between the *species intelligibilis* as that **by which** one knows and the actual object **known** provides a useful contrast.\n\n**Method:** Both text families apply a common scholastic discipline: distinguish senses before deciding whether an objection defeats a doctrine. A modern synthesis should retain this **method of determination**, including its technical distinctions, rather than treat medieval kalām as a set of anti-Peripatetic slogans.\n\n## VII. Critical status and further source needs\n\n**Actually translated:** Selected arguments and objections from primary online editions of *Mawāqif* VII:34–35,45 and *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid* pp.78–79, including the theologians' trilemma, proof from continuity, mental-existence objections and the differentiating reply. **Not translated in full:** all interleaved Siyālkūtī and Ḥalabī glosses, all of the original chapter context, and the complete original *Tajrīd* parallel.\n\n**Not claimed:** A fully authenticated attribution of each parenthetical/glossed comment in the multi-author *Mawāqif* print, fresh independent manuscript collation, or an original verified AT thesis in Aquinas on every particular kalām objection.\n\n**Further work needed:** Compare the natural-philosophy chapters of *al-Mabāḥith al-mashriqiyya*, *Sharḥ al-Mawāqif*, *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*, *Shawāriq*, and the late Indian manuals including Khayrābādī's *Hadiyya*. The result should identify precisely when an argument for matter became stronger, became circular, or was replaced by an alternative analysis.\n"},{"id":"ch10","kind":"analysis","author":"GNAMMA scholarly editorial synthesis","work":"Late Avicennian Summa · comparative chapters","title":"Conservation and the First Cause","discipline":"Metaphysics","locus":"Working chapter","lang":"mixed","family":"Comparative analysis","coverage":"Working analytical chapter with selected primary passages","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/gnamma-research/booksite/blob/master/research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/10-conservation-first-cause-and-secondary-agency.md","sourceFile":"research/intellectual-atlas/avicennian-summa/chapters/10-conservation-first-cause-and-secondary-agency.md","topics":["conservation","causation","divine-will"],"sourceCoverage":"Selected Arabic/Latin excerpts; no full primary witness","manuscriptCollated":false,"md":"# Book IV, Chapter 3 — Creation, conservation, and the hierarchy of secondary causes\n**Ibn Sīnā, *al-Shifāʾ: al-Ilāhiyyāt* VI.1–2; Narāqī, *Jāmiʿ al-afkār* I:38–42; Aquinas, *Summa theologiae* I qq.104–105**\n\n**Editorial status:** A **completed comparative argument chapter** centred on verified primary passages and their logical relationship. The Arabic and Latin extracts translated below are *selected lemmata*, not a continuous edition of every sentence of the three source texts. Their whole objections and replies are analysed as connected arguments and explicitly marked as paraphrase where not translated. No manuscript inspection or new witness-level collation is claimed.\n\n**Verified source locations:**\n- Ibn Sīnā, [*Ilāhiyyāt* VI.1 pp.259–260](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/260), [VI.1 p.261](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/261), [VI.1 p.262](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/262) and [VI.2 p.264](https://books.rafed.net/view/1128/page/264). **Edition-specific qualification:** in this printing the preservation objection starts on p.261 **before** the VI.2 chapter heading at p.264; do not misattribute p.261 as VI.2 merely because it addresses efficient causality.\n- Narāqī, [*Jāmiʿ al-afkār* I:38–40](https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=728&page=39&type=c_fbook) and [I:41–42](https://lib.rafed.net/view.php?b_id=728&page=41&type=c_fbook).\n- Aquinas, [*Summa theologiae* I q.104 aa.1–3, corpus and responses](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html), particularly corpus markers **[32762]**, **[32771]**, and **[32779]**.\n\n## I. Why this chapter belongs between *jaʿl* and divine agency\n\nIn the previous chapters, Lāhījī asks **what the cause makes**: not the abstract concept of existence, nor an essence imagined to be already actual, but this particular existent, understood in different respects. The natural-philosophy chapters then distinguish natural generation, the intrinsic form of a composite, and the Active Intellect's bestowal of forms. These discussions raise a third problem: **what sustains a substance once generated?**\n\nThe question cannot be answered by confusing three different meanings of “cause”:\n\n1. **A cause of becoming**: an agent that produces a transition or arrangement, such as a builder arranging materials.\n2. **A cause of form**: an agent or principle responsible for the presence of a constitutive substantial form.\n3. **A cause of continuing being**: a cause upon which the actuality of an effect depends *as long as that effect exists*.\n\nA single cause may occupy more than one role. It does **not** follow that every natural generator performs all three, nor that there is exactly one cause occupying each role.\n\n## II. Ibn Sīnā: the cause explains existence, not merely “after nonexistence”\n\n### II.1. What is given by the efficient agent?\n\n**Arabic lemma — *Ilāhiyyāt*, printed p.260**\n\n> إنما له من الفاعل وجوده.\n\n**Working translation:** What the effect receives from its efficient cause is **its existence**.\n\n**Contextual argument — editorial exposition:** Ibn Sīnā distinguishes (a) *the effect's existence*, (b) *its previously not existing*, and (c) *its being now existent after a period of nonexistence*. Only the first is, precisely as such, the positive actuality received from the efficient source. The fact of prior nonexistence is not a positive effect manufactured by that source. The causal relation is therefore not adequately defined as the bringing about of the *temporal relation “after”*.\n\n**Interpretive consequence:** Even an eternal contingent effect could, in this technical sense, need a cause for its existence. Whether such an eternal created world is possible is a *further* thesis; the modal analysis does not itself prove a created cosmos has no first temporal moment.\n\n### II.2. The preservation objection is already Avicennian\n\n**Arabic lemma — p.261, an objection Ibn Sīnā reports**\n\n> فظن من ظن أن الشيء إنما يحتاج إلى العلة في حدوثه، فإذا حدث ووجد فقد استغنى عن العلة.\n\n**Working translation:** Some supposed that a thing needs its cause only in its origination, and that once it has originated and exists, it has become independent of the cause.\n\nIbn Sīnā immediately rejects that inference. Consider the effect *now existing*:\n\n- Either it is necessary through its own essence, in which case it was not contingent simply in virtue of having originated.\n- Or its continued existence is necessary under some condition, which must be analysed to discover whether that condition is the past event of origination, an intrinsic attribute, or an independently existing determinant.\n- **Temporal origination cannot supply the needed necessity**. Origination is itself contingent and, as a past description, cannot by itself explain an effect's actuality now.\n- If the supposed intrinsic characteristic makes the essence necessary in itself, the original classification of that effect as contingent has been abandoned rather than explained.\n- A merely further dependent characteristic sends us back to the causal question; it does not eliminate it.\n\n### II.3. The closing premise from p.262\n\n**Arabic lemma**\n\n> فلا دخول للعدم المتقدم في أن يكون للوجود الحادث علة.\n\n**Working translation:** The preceding nonexistence plays no role in making the originated existent require a cause.\n\nThe primary source continues by affirming that the kind of being in question requires a cause **even if it continues and persists**. This is a stronger textual foundation for the modern comparison than simply assuming that every medieval philosopher believed that temporal creation and conservation were two independent events.\n\n### II.4. From VI.1 to VI.2: builders, fathers and heat\n\nThe next chapter, **VI.2**, begins at printed p.264, with an objection: builders die and their houses remain; fathers die and their children remain; fire can depart while heat persists.\n\n**Arabic lemma — VI.2, p.264**\n\n> فإن البناء والأب والنار ليست عللا بالحقيقة لقوام هذه المعلولات.\n\n**Working translation:** For the builder, the father and the fire are not, strictly speaking, causes of the continuing constitution of these effects.\n\n**The argument:** These agents are causes of specific **motions and preparatory transformations**. A builder moves materials and produces an arrangement; a father is a causal condition of reproductive processes; a fire produces physical alterations in a patient. The essential and existential dependence of the resulting composite may have a different set of causes from the causes of those earlier transformations.\n\n**Crucial qualification:** This does **not** imply that fire cannot be a genuine secondary cause of heating or that builders do not really construct buildings. Ibn Sīnā's precise claim is about **whether their previous actions are the continuing ontological cause of the already established result**.\n\n**Difference between philosophical and everyday causal speech:** “The father caused the son” is appropriate in ordinary speech. In the causal analysis, “father” names a participant in an earlier process, not automatically the simultaneous cause upon which the son's present existing depends.\n\n## III. Narāqī: the late defence by the essence's enduring contingency\n\nNarāqī's fourth preliminary in *Jāmiʿ al-afkār*, I:38–42, explicitly states the same principle. His contribution is **not discovering conservation for the first time**. He gives a connected modal and epistemological demonstration, alongside objections from the theological tradition.\n\n### III.1. Ground of need: contingency rather than origination\n\n**Arabic lemma — printed p.38**\n\n> الحقّ أن علة احتياج الممكن إلى المؤثّر هو الإمكان.\n\n**Working translation:** The correct view is that **contingency** is the ground of a possible being's need for an efficient cause.\n\nThe argument depends on the principle that, if existence and nonexistence are equally compatible with the essence of a contingent being, their actual determination requires an external determinant (*murajjiḥ*). One must distinguish **not requiring either alternative** from the stronger (and contradictory) suggestion that an essence somehow positively requires both alternatives.\n\n### III.2. The priority argument\n\nNarāqī argues that making temporal origination the ground of causal need leads to a cycle in explanatory priority:\n\n1. Origination presupposes an existent with a prior lack of existence.\n2. That existent's actuality presupposes production.\n3. Production presupposes the effect's dependence upon an efficient source.\n4. The ground of that dependence must be prior **in the order of explanation**.\n5. If temporal origination were the ground, the result of this order would have to be prior to its own explanatory prerequisites.\n\nThese are **ontological and epistemic ranks**, not elapsed moments measured on a clock.\n\n### III.3. Why the distinction matters for persistence\n\n**Arabic lemma — printed p.39**\n\n> الإمكان لازم لماهية الممكن ذاتي لها لا ينفكّ عنها حال البقاء أيضاً.\n\n**Working translation:** Contingency is intrinsic to a possible quiddity and does not depart from it even in the state of continuance.\n\nTherefore the reason for causal dependence is still present while the effect exists. Narāqī also meets the argument that continued conservation would be *taḥṣīl al-ḥāṣil*, a useless second conferment of existence already obtained: **being at an earlier time does not by itself account for the continuance of being at a later time**.\n\n**Interpretive caution:** This need not mean that a new numerically different substance is continuously created at each instant. One must not smuggle occasionalism into an Avicennian conservation argument.\n\n## IV. Aquinas: created causes can genuinely conserve\n\nThis is an **important corrective** to simplified modern comparisons.\n\n### IV.1. Conservation is a distinct explanatory dependence, not an additional temporal creation\n\nIn *Summa theologiae* I q.104 a.1, Aquinas distinguishes protection **against corruption** from direct conservation through the cause upon which a being depends. Every creature requires the latter from God, even if a particular creature has no ordinary natural cause of corruption.\n\n**Latin lemma — q.104 a.1 ad 4 [32766]**\n\n> conservatio rerum a Deo non est per aliquam novam actionem; sed per continuationem actionis qua dat esse.\n\n**Working translation:** God's preservation of things is not a fresh action superadded to the first; it is the continuance of the action by which he gives them being.\n\nThis rebuts the assumption that conservation must mean repeated, separate temporal creations. It also explains why the first cause can continually support an effect whose intrinsic natural powers persist and operate.\n\n### IV.2. The builder analogy: exact convergence and precise difference\n\n**Latin lemma — q.104 a.1 corpus [32762]**\n\n> Aedificator enim est causa domus quantum ad eius fieri, non autem directe quantum ad esse eius.\n\n**Working translation:** A builder causes a house **in its coming-to-be**, but is not directly the cause of the house **with respect to its being**.\n\nThis is strikingly close to Ibn Sīnā's example in *Ilāhiyyāt* VI.2. But do not conflate their surrounding metaphysics. Aquinas explains the continuing house by the arrangement's dependence upon the natural qualities of the building materials; Ibn Sīnā embeds the example in a cosmology in which the ultimate bestowers of elemental substantial forms are separate principles. The *analogy* is shared, but the causal allocation behind it differs.\n\n### IV.3. The underappreciated article: **secondary conservation** (q.104 a.2)\n\nAquinas explicitly says that creatures may be conserving causes of other creatures. He distinguishes mere indirect conservation (e.g. shielding something against a corrupting agent) from **direct conservation by an ordered cause** whose influence helps sustain an effect.\n\n**Latin lemma — q.104 a.2 corpus [32771]**\n\n> necesse est quod effectus dependeat primo quidem et principaliter a causa prima; secundario vero ab omnibus causis mediis.\n\n**Working translation:** The effect must depend first and principally on the First Cause, and secondarily upon all the intermediary causes.\n\nHe even says that in some cases God preserves effects *through* intermediary causes, while his own principal conservation remains presupposed. Therefore:\n\n**“Avicenna allows mediation, Aquinas allows only immediate causality” is false as a blanket historical claim.**\n\nAquinas rejects an account in which **separate intellects are autonomous creators** or the First's free act is constrained by cosmic necessitation. He does **not** deny that created causes can exercise a conserving influence in their proper order.\n\n### IV.4. Created generation and conservation are still constrained\n\nAquinas q.104 a.2 ad 3 distinguishes introducing a new form or disposition by change from preserving the resulting determination without a further change, as in the continued illumination of air by a present light source. The same created cause can therefore be causally relevant **after** the initial transition, but not because it has become a self-sufficient source of being.\n\nThis is closer to the layered Avicennian picture than an exclusively immediate-causation model would be.\n\n### IV.5. The decisive outstanding disagreement: willing versus natural necessity\n\nIn q.104 a.3, Aquinas says God preserves the creature because he **wills** to continue imparting existence, and that the First's natural simplicity does not force the production of every conceivable effect. The Avicennian thesis about the completeness of a cause and consequent necessity cannot simply be equated with this theological conclusion.\n\nThus, agreement on:\n- causal dependence,\n- simultaneous ordering of preserving causes,\n- conservation without repeated temporal creation,\n\ndoes **not** prove agreement concerning:\n- free specification of a cosmic order,\n- how many intermediary intellects exist or what functions they perform,\n- whether the First could refrain from creating this world,\n- the necessity and individuation of substantial forms.\n\n## V. A revised comparison matrix\n\n| Question | Ibn Sīnā | Narāqī | Aquinas | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Does a dependent being require a source beyond the event of origination? | Explicit, VI.1 p.261–262 | Explicit, I:38–42 | Explicit, I q.104 a.1 | **Strong convergent doctrine** |\n| Does the previous nonexistence explain causal need? | No | No | Not the primary criterion; specifies dependence on cause of *esse* | Strong convergence, distinct framework |\n| Is the builder the continued source of the house's existence? | No, not in the strict sense | No, in analogous discussion | No, not *per se* with respect to *esse* | **Genuinely striking parallel** |\n| Can secondary causes be real agents? | Yes; precise division of natural and separate causes | Yes, implied by argument; detailed natural theory uncollated | Yes | Shared commitment, **different allocation** |\n| Can some intermediary created causes help conserve effects? | Yes, in an ordered cosmology | Needs precise collation of other works | **Yes, expressly q.104 a.2** | Earlier project simplification corrected |\n| Are all finite beings numerically identical with the First? | No | Rejects strong readings of such identity | No | Shared real plurality, different theories |\n| Does divine simplicity force this cosmos to proceed necessarily? | Emanation and necessity profoundly relevant | Specific complete doctrine requires further source comparison | Rejects a necessarily chosen cosmos | **Major unresolved disagreement** |\n| Is the universal Giver of Forms required for all natural generation? | Classical bestowal role | Historical text still to collate | No in ordinary generation | Central causal difference remains |\n| Does conservation imply the absence of continuous substantial motion? | No; separate issue | Not decided here | No; separate question | Avoid invalid inference |\n\n## VI. The strongest constructive thesis consistent with the texts\n\n**Proposed synthesis (not an attributed medieval doctrine):**\n\nAn actual natural substance has intrinsic constitutive principles (matter, form and nature), real efficient and final powers, and an identity that persists across many activities and changes. Its causal dependence may include many ordered secondary causes, while none of these causes—singly or collectively—is the unconditioned source of its existing. Its continuous dependence on a First Cause is distinct from both its initial natural generation and the temporal succession of its accidental activities.\n\nThis proposal **does not automatically dissolve** the difference concerning the separate Giver of Forms, or the difference between necessary cosmic emanation and freely willed creation.\n\n### Four diagnostic tests for a genuinely coherent AT–Avicennian reconstruction\n\n1. **Ontology test:** Is the quiddity/existence analysis applied to one *actual* existent without introducing a pre-existing essence?\n2. **Natural causation test:** Which agent is the efficient source of the *substantial composite*, not merely of preparatory motions?\n3. **Conservation test:** Are the First Cause's universal action and intermediary sustaining causes distinguished without treating them as rival agencies?\n4. **Freedom test:** Is the cause/effect necessity *conditional upon* a fully specified cause, or does it entail that this entire contingent world proceeds from the First without free choice?\n\nA theory may pass tests 1 and 3 and fail 2 or 4. It cannot be declared “equivalent to AT” on the strength of conservation alone.\n\n## VII. Exact integration with the first nine chapters\n\n| Earlier chapter | The proposition this chapter needs | What the current chapter supplies |\n|---|---|---|\n| 01 — Mental being | The concept of existence is not an extra physical property | Causal actuality is not the attachment of an “existence-object” |\n| 02 — *Jaʿl* | Particular existence and the meaning of simple making | Explains *why* such particular actuality remains dependent |\n| 03 — Substantial motion | Identity of the actual subject is controversial | Shows conservation does not decide whether existence itself moves |\n| 04 — Intellect and soul | A separate intellect's epistemic role differs from its cosmic role | Prevents conflation of intellectual illumination with conserving being |\n| 05 — Four causes | Intrinsic form, proximate generator and separate bestower | Adds distinct cause of *continued* being |\n| 06 — Divine agency | Causal necessity is not freely choosing this very cosmic order | Uses Aquinas q.104 to sharpen the disputed inference |\n| 07 — Unity and knowledge | Divine causal containment does not entail numerical monism | Keeps the First and dependent beings distinct |\n| 08 — Universals and quiddity | The absolute consideration of essence is not a separate actuality | Explains the contingency of *instantiated* beings |\n| 09 — Kalām | Multiple senses of causation and existence produce spurious disagreements | Supplies an example of sorting those senses before adjudication |\n\n## VIII. Textual integrity and next-stage requirements\n\n**New work actually delivered:** a direct Ibn Sīnā–Narāqī–Aquinas comparison based on verified source passages, with multiple original lemma translations, an argument-by-argument reconstruction, and a corrected account of Aquinas on mediated conservation.\n\n**Not falsely claimed:** a full diplomatic edition of *Ilāhiyyāt* VI.1–2; an unabridged translation of *Jāmiʿ* I:38–42; a complete comparison of the *Tajrīd* and *Mawāqif* on conservation; manuscript readings inferred from printed sigla.\n\n**Future philological priority:** Arabic p.260–264 in their entirety with a proper variant table; the complete Narāqī fourth preliminary, preserving its Persian couplet and all objections; independent Latin edition of Aquinas q.104 a.1–2; and actual late form-bestowal passages in Jilwa and Khayrābādī.\n\nThis chapter **completes the current analytic comparison**, not the twenty-domain critical translation of all primary works.\n"}],"themes":[{"id":"method","num":1,"title":"The subject and method of metaphysics","summary":"How being qua being, first notions and demonstration delimit metaphysical science.","records":["ch01","nara-second","ch08","av-vi1"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"existence","num":2,"title":"Existence and quiddity","summary":"Actual existence, essence, making and predication must be kept apart.","records":["nara-actual","lah-shaw","ch01","ch02","av-vi1"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"unity","num":3,"title":"Unity, plurality and gradation of existence","summary":"A common concept, gradation of existences and strong monism are distinct claims.","records":["lah-eman","nara-actual","ch07"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"mental-existence","num":4,"title":"Mental existence and nafs al-amr","summary":"An intelligible object's status is not identical to extra-mental actuality.","records":["taft-mental","jurj-mental","nara-second","ch01","ch08"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"causation","num":5,"title":"The four causes and effective agency","summary":"Material, formal, efficient and final causes are related but not interchangeable.","records":["av-vi1","av-vi2","ch05","lah-divine"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"forms","num":6,"title":"The Giver of Forms","summary":"Physical preparation, separate bestowal, and intrinsic substantial form.","records":["av-ix5","lah-active","ch05"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"matter","num":7,"title":"Hylomorphism and individuation","summary":"What remains through loss of form, and whether matter exists apart from form.","records":["ch09","av-ix5","lah-active","ch03"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"motion","num":8,"title":"Motion and substantial change","summary":"Compare discontinuous formal generation with Ṣadrian substantial motion.","records":["ch03","khay-fire","lah-temporal"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"modality","num":9,"title":"Necessity and sufficiency of a cause","summary":"Contingency in itself and necessity through another; divine choosing.","records":["lah-shaw","astar-choice","ch06","av-vi1"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"conservation","num":10,"title":"Creation and continuous conservation","summary":"A cause of coming to be may differ from an abiding cause of being.","records":["av-vi1","av-vi2","lah-temporal","lah-sarmaya","aq-104a","aq-104b","ch10"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"cosmology","num":11,"title":"Separate intellects and cosmic mediation","summary":"The tenth intellect and the classical layered celestial explanation.","records":["av-ix5","lah-eman","lah-active","ch06"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"soul","num":12,"title":"Origination of the rational soul","summary":"Distinguish soul's originating relation to body from species form.","records":["lah-active","ch04"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"knowledge","num":13,"title":"Abstraction and intellect","summary":"The Active Intellect's epistemic function versus its natural role.","records":["ch04","ch08","taft-mental","jurj-mental"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"imagination","num":14,"title":"Imagination and the afterlife","summary":"Internal senses, embodied cognition and survival; source coverage remains partial.","records":["ch04","ch07"],"completeness":"research-gap"},{"id":"divine-knowledge","num":15,"title":"Divine knowledge of particulars","summary":"Causal knowledge, divine simplicity and particular events.","records":["ch06","ch07"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"divine-will","num":16,"title":"Divine simplicity, will and power","summary":"Riḍā, qaṣd, necessary procession, alternative possibility and freedom.","records":["lah-divine","astar-choice","aq-104b","ch06"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"providence","num":17,"title":"Providence, evil and finality","summary":"Purposiveness, permission and a divine causal order.","records":["lah-divine","ch06","aq-104b"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"proofs","num":18,"title":"Proofs of a First Cause and regress","summary":"Essential dependence must be distinguished from indefinite temporal succession.","records":["av-vi1","av-vi2","lah-shaw","ch10"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"universals","num":19,"title":"Universals, logic and second intentions","summary":"A universal qua universal is intelligible without being an external universal object.","records":["nara-second","taft-mental","ch08","ch01"],"completeness":"partial-primary-evidence"},{"id":"practical","num":20,"title":"Ethics, political order and the human good","summary":"Not yet source-complete; avoid deducing institutions from metaphysics alone.","records":["ch04","ch07"],"completeness":"research-gap"}],"debates":[{"id":"giver","index":1,"question":"Does a natural generator confer substantial form?","summary":"Classical Avicenna assigns preparation and separate form-bestowal to distinct causal levels; Lāhījī explicitly preserves the tenth intellect.","records":["av-ix5","lah-active","ch05"],"caution":"The Aquinas comparison remains a real dispute about proximate generation, not whether forms are intrinsic."},{"id":"dependence","index":2,"question":"Why does a contingent thing need its cause while it exists?","summary":"Existential conservation is different from temporal origination or the artisan's past work.","records":["av-vi1","av-vi2","lah-sarmaya","aq-104a","ch10"],"caution":"Parallel claims of dependence do not demonstrate identity of creation or free willing."},{"id":"one","index":3,"question":"How does the One give rise to many?","summary":"One immediate effect, mediated respects and the lunar Active Intellect form a hierarchical explanation.","records":["lah-eman","lah-active","ch06"],"caution":"This is Lāhījī's reported philosophical system, not empirically vindicated astronomy."},{"id":"temporal","index":4,"question":"How can an eternal cause explain a new event?","summary":"Ontological cause and time-specifying preparation do different explanatory work.","records":["av-vi2","lah-temporal","ch10"],"caution":"Retain the classical celestial assumptions as historically contingent."},{"id":"choice","index":5,"question":"Does necessity through will abolish genuine choice?","summary":"Qūshjī, Khafrī and later scholastics distinguish conditional necessity from strong alternative possibilities.","records":["lah-shaw","astar-choice","lah-divine","aq-104b"],"caution":"The earlier voices in Astarābādī are mediated reports, not independent text witnesses."},{"id":"making","index":6,"question":"Does an agent make quiddity or particular existence?","summary":"Simple and compound jaʿl and the distinction between essence as such and an actual individual.","records":["lah-shaw","taha-jal","nara-actual","ch02"],"caution":"Mīr Zāhid is accessed through Tahānawī rather than an original independently collated ḥāshiya."},{"id":"mind","index":7,"question":"Does understanding heat make the mind hot?","summary":"Taftāzānī and Jurjānī separate external reality from the mental form of what is known.","records":["taft-mental","jurj-mental","nara-second","ch01"],"caution":"Anonymous marginal material cannot be assigned to named glossators without inspection."},{"id":"motion","index":8,"question":"Can substance move without losing identity?","summary":"Lāhījī presses persistence; Ṣadrā proposes continuity of existence; later Indian natural change offers related but distinct questions.","records":["ch03","khay-fire","av-ix5"],"caution":"The Indian quotation is mediated and not a proof of the separate Giver of Forms."},{"id":"concept","index":9,"question":"Are quiddity and existence two extra-mental things?","summary":"Some later writers affirm conceptual difference without two independent external entities.","records":["nara-actual","ch01","ch02","lah-eman"],"caution":"Do not equate Narāqī's position with Aquinas' real composition without argument."},{"id":"created","index":10,"question":"Can God cease to conserve a creature?","summary":"Aquinas distinguishes ability to withhold being from a claim that any creature is annihilated.","records":["aq-104a","aq-104b","lah-divine","ch06"],"caution":"Avicenna's causal necessity should not be mapped directly onto Thomist freedom."}],"sourcePolicy":{"critical":"The English texts are working translations and interpretive summaries from edited witnesses; Arabic, Persian and Latin text is quoted as anchors only. Do not regard a passage as a fully aligned bilingual text unless the source is actually transcribed.","direct":"Some records report earlier authors within later texts. These are not independent collations of the named author.","comparative":"Comparisons are scholarly editorial syntheses, not statements attributed to historical authors."}});
